select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "dc125570-e1fd-460f-8e98-f4d710b0315c", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Brian Seales (09:17): Conversations with the client directly? I think that I spend a lot of time with the clients to try to understand their personality and sometimes try to corner 'em into situations where they're uncomfortable so that they can be honest about what they're trying to do. (09:29): And like I said before, it's based on the consumer response. So everything that I ask our clients about the personality they're trying to create for their business or what they prefer to create for their business is based on what I think that target audience is going to respond to. And so I'll push back a little bit to say, maybe this isn't the best option for this audience, and go from there. And a lot of times the businesses are providing a very specific service that doesn't require any type of character creation or mascots or anything like that, but they still want to inject them into their branding for one reason or another, like a cat in your logo. And I'll talk them out of that if I don't think it's appropriate for that. But you're right, we aren't experts in that area, and we know that they know their business better than us. So we are very collaborative with our conversations with our clients to make sure that we're always on the same page. So early concepts will be like, this is why we did what we did. Tell us how you feel about it, what you like and dislike, and we'll talk about it more to make sure that we're not doing something that isn't going to be successful. Because really, again, we just want to be successful. Early on, we would allow our clients to kind of bully us into doing certain things, (10:42): And we didn't think we'd get the same response out of it. So now we don't allow that. We never give our clients anything that we don't think is going to be successful in the marketplace.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:49): I see. So you're looking out for their best interest.\n\nBrian Seales (10:52): Absolutely. You have to, even if it's hard to have that conversation with them from time to time.", "token_estimate": 506, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:17", "end_timestamp": "10:52", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "5b585057-a735-4049-b48e-836daf57a25b", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:57): One thing that I've noticed in my conversations with clients is sometimes they'll come to you and they think that they want, in my case, a video or a project. And in sitting across the table from them and having a conversation, it's very apparent that maybe they don't need a video. Maybe what they need is a brand strategy, or maybe they need a website. Do you come across that often?\n\nBrian Seales (11:17): Yeah, a lot. Actually. I prefer that our clients say, we want to get this kind of return. Tell us how we can do that instead of, we're going to do a print ad in this magazine. I mean, that's fine. We can do a print ad for you, but it doesn't necessarily mean that that's the end result that you're hoping for. If it's a marketing director at a company that's large enough to accommodate that, then that's fine. I trust that. But if it's someone who just said, this X publication contacted us and wants us to do this summer guide and it's going to cost us $4,500 to do it, I will say, why are you doing, let's have a conversation about why you're doing that instead of other options that are out there. So we definitely have their best interest in mind. I don't want them to say, we spent 4,500 on this ad and it wasn't successful. If I haven't told them, I don't think that's the best fit.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:05): Maybe you've never had that conversation about what they're trying to achieve.\n\nBrian Seales (12:07): Yeah, right.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:08): Exactly. So starting with the business goal in mind and then figuring out a solution from there is how you approach.\n\nBrian Seales (12:14): Absolutely. I mean, if a client comes into us and asks us that first, I know we're going to be a really good fit.\n\nSpeaker 3 (12:20): If\n\nBrian Seales (12:20): They say we need a logo, you probably do. But if you need something else, we need a website. We will just talk about what the return on that investment is going to be because our services cost money,\n\nCole Heilborn (12:31): And\n\nBrian Seales (12:31): I want to make sure that they're not looking at us as purely as an expense. It has to be an investment.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:36): So", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Brian Seales", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "10:57", "end_timestamp": "12:36", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "8bb9e189-e239-412d-974a-4b2917a55236", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Brian Seales (12:36): We'll have those tough conversations. Yeah, we do talk people out of working with us from time to time, but it's better to do that ahead of time than have to explain it three months later. It's like, yeah, well, you really shouldn't have done that anyway.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:47): Right. Yeah. I guess last thing you want to do is finish a project and then they're disappointed and the expectations are missed because you didn't have the right conversation at the beginning.\n\nBrian Seales (12:55): Yes.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:56): Yeah, that's tough. But I think that's part of ethics of doing business, right?\n\nBrian Seales (13:00): And we're at a point now where we have enough volume that we can do that and not be too concerned about whether we'll be busy the next month. It's not just like, who's going to pay our next rent bill? It's more about developing long-term relationships with people and having them trust us as an organization.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:17): So in, I guess the global sphere when it comes to brands, are there any brands that you follow or that you're fan of or that you're, you enjoy seeing what they're doing?\n\nBrian Seales (13:31): There are a lot of brands that I appreciate. I actually gravitate towards following the ones that I don't like as much, and I'm not going to call them out. I just am interested in how they evaluate themselves. I try to get away from, I try to avoid, I definitely follow brands that encourage any type of toxic masculinity, because I think it's ridiculous if you use brewery branding as NASCAR style men outside football, that kind of thing. It's just like, why would you want to ignore a huge audience that would appreciate what you're doing if it's so niched into that kind of a thing? I'm a big fan of big global brands. None that I think are doing well, a lot, do an amazing job, but nothing that I'm following specifically right now.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:19): Okay. Are there specific campaigns or brands that you have seen come through the advertising world and you marked that as something that you appreciate or something that was done well in your opinion?", "token_estimate": 535, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:36", "end_timestamp": "14:19", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "4ef1393a-6be3-46d0-bb43-238ae31cc1ee", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Brian Seales (14:34): Yeah. You're kind of putting me on the spot to figure one of these things out, but there've been so many that it's, I have a hard time recalling anything in particular.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:44): Okay.\n\nBrian Seales (14:47): We'll fill in the blanks later if I get in, if I figure\n\nCole Heilborn (14:49): That one out, that's fair. We'll add it in the text description, the\n\nBrian Seales (14:51): Podcast. Okay. That's right. That's perfect. Yeah, I'm sure it'll hit me tonight at one o'clock in the morning.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:54): That's right. Oh, that's the one\n\nBrian Seales (14:56): Gentle cold.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:59): I imagine you guys work with companies of varying sizes with varying customer pools. Some brands I'm sure work with 10 customers a year and some work with thousands, hundreds of thousands potentially. Right?\n\nBrian Seales (15:11): Correct.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:13): Coming back full circle to one of my original questions is why, again, is that brand important when you're working with a customer base, as large as some of these brands out there are working with hundreds of thousands of customers, why is that important? Why do these brands spend so much money on developing their brand and developing content that supports it and communicates it", "token_estimate": 323, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "14:34", "end_timestamp": "15:13", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "dc14ac34-2779-4c4f-8f47-ecb4739ca818", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Brian Seales (15:36): Well for the consistency for sure, it provides credibility to the product or service that they have. If a consumer base can see value in the energy that a company is putting into a brand, then they will likely trust it more. And then when you're talking about large consumer groups like that, if you set up the toolbox to allow your consumer base to distribute the information on your behalf just as a fan base, then that's a huge audience that is just promoting your product or service for free. And so if you give them those options or you create an interesting point of view or character or something that's shareable, and I know content is really important in a brand right now, let's take advantage of social media and just say there is a huge opportunity for people to promote your products on your behalf. If you do something that's either polarizing or perhaps just trendy and shooting from the hip that they're like, I like this. I'm going to tell all my friends about it. Having a brand concept in place that allows for that scalability where your content writers can say, yeah, we're still within our branding guidelines and we're going to do this interesting campaign within that helps keep everything in the same package essentially, so that when someone shares it, (16:56): It's not confusing.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:57): It's\n\nBrian Seales (16:57): Not. It's like, oh yeah, this is Coca-Cola or whoever. Even though it's different from what they normally do, I can really understand that they're still on brand. No consumer's going to say, yeah, this company's on brand. You have to be a huge nerd like me to say that. But they'll get it intuitively. They'll be like, I know what this is.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:13): How many times have you said that in conversation on\n\nBrian Seales (17:15): Brand? Yeah, probably at least 20,000.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:20): So what do you think is the deciding factor, or what is that? How does a customer look at a brand and make that decision that they want to share it with a friend or a family member?", "token_estimate": 517, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:36", "end_timestamp": "17:20", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "b3bcfaa7-db11-41f5-8b76-d5993d9a7c92", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Brian Seales (17:30): Well, usually in those cases, it has a personal relationship to them. It's not talking about the difference between push and pull marketing tactics. And if you push your product and say, this product is the best product because it does these things, (17:47): Usually it doesn't have as good of a response as encouraging people to use it in a different way or explaining the personality of the company and allowing the consumer to decide whether they want that product or not. Large product lines, Coca-Cola never says, I don't know why I'm using them. They're huge, I suppose, but they never say they're famous drink coke because it's delicious and it makes your throat sting a bit when you drink it, but only drink it when it's cold. If it gets warm, it's going to be really bad. I mean, they don't go through that. They just kind of show people doing things differently, showing people walking down the street in a giant parade, being happy and energetic, their brand is happy and energetic, and then people will gravitate with that, even if they're not, they're like, yeah, you know what? I want to be happy and energetic, so I'm going to drink Coke now. And that's a pull marketing tactic. Instead of just saying, this is why you want it, this is become part of this huge organization or this huge community. So yeah, I'm not sure I'm answering that question.", "token_estimate": 347, "speakers": ["Brian Seales"], "start_timestamp": "17:30", "end_timestamp": "17:30", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "7dfacaba-75ca-48b2-bce3-d4230d0f4298", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:48): No, I think you are. And it's funny you mentioned that because earlier today I was having a client call and we were going over a proposal that we were working on together, and the client ended up saying, we're not quite how we're trying to sell ourselves. And they said in the past, we've tried to sell ourselves based on our product that it's high quality, that it's made well, that it's fill in the blank. And he said, we've come to the realization that that's not how we should be selling ourselves. We should be selling fill in the blank, something else, but they're not quite sure what that is. And so they said, we're not ready to do this project because we're still trying to figure that out. And I was like, yes, you should. That's a good thing to figure out. Is that where you guys can step in and help them resolve that?\n\nBrian Seales (19:34): And we've actually worked with a lot of local companies or a few local companies that are resistant to that point of view, and they haven't been remarkably successful doing the features and benefits kind of promotion, which existed in the sixties and seventies when it was more like, this is a quality thing and you should have it because of these reasons. Like, okay, thanks for the facts. I'll buy this thing. It's changed. It's evolved into being more lifestyle driven and being more, this fits into your life in one way or another. And so determining your client's going to have a challenging time figuring out which niche they can use to be the most effective.\n\nSpeaker 3 (20:09): But\n\nBrian Seales (20:09): We do jump in and try to organize what common denominators are involved with the target audience so that we can kind of prey on those bit. I mean, we don't want to shift the personality of the brand to accommodate that,\n\nCole Heilborn (20:22): But", "token_estimate": 460, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Brian Seales", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "18:48", "end_timestamp": "20:22", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "b1cbb314-e961-4600-b734-5d6a79095c04", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Brian Seales (20:22): There are certain ways you can promote the way an automotive, depending on the type of car that's being promoted, you'll either see them going through the hills or you'll see them going through the city. That's a lifestyle promotion. They don't say It's really easy to park this in the city. They don't have to. They're just showing the city instead. So that kind of campaign was still being on brand is really, really important. 20,000 in one times now\n\nCole Heilborn (20:50): For why are customers drawn towards the lifestyle side of a brand and making a purchase through that rather than being sold on the benefits, which obviously play a role, but they're sometimes deeper in the sales cycle that that's when a customer starts to look at the specifics to an item or a purchase. Right.\n\nBrian Seales (21:16): So I'm looking for a bed right now, and there's so many online mattress companies that are buying exactly the same product, in my opinion. I don't know if that's true or not. And then marketing them differently based on their audience. And so at the end of the day, I don't have a long story about that. I'm just using that as an example. The product has to be good enough to support the sale. So at the end of the day, you can't sell snake oil to people. You make the lifestyle as amazing as possible. There's a product out right now called Bang Energy, which is really jumping on a lot of Instagram influencers. It's probably the worst thing you could ever buy. I don't know that I've never had it, so I can't say that for sure. But it just seems like it's an unnecessary thing to, it's\n\nCole Heilborn (22:04): Like an energy drink type.\n\nBrian Seales (22:05): It's an energy drink.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:06): Gotcha.", "token_estimate": 438, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:22", "end_timestamp": "22:06", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "b732864a-6d83-4664-9f9a-3a797c3e28e4", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Brian Seales (22:07): It's huge too. They have these Instagram influencers that are promoting it now. I'm seeing it more and more in supermarkets, and I think it's a trendy thing that'll disappear, but they're kind of taking advantage of the lifestyle thing and saying, oh, you followed this person on Instagram and they're drinking this energy drink, which they probably just carrying it around, and then it just kind of convinces you to go and buy it. And then you get one and you're like, oh my gosh, this is horrible. I don't ever want this again. And then the campaign falls off. And so they might be just in that market where they founded a 55 gallon drum of energy drink, and they're like, we got to move this. Let's just get rid of it.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:48): Throw a name on\n\nBrian Seales (22:48): It. Energy. Yeah, let's do it. Which we can help with that too. But yeah, I think that just taking advantage of lifestyle is just a benefit. I mean, it is the best way to get to a consumer audience, but the product has to follow itself up.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:05): Sure. Because people want to see themselves using that product because they want to see what their life could be like. Is that the idea?\n\nBrian Seales (23:13): Yeah, definitely. I mean, the Fitbit is one of those products that just kind of took off because of the lifestyle point of view. All it is, it's just a little thing that's counting your steps, but it's like the lifestyle. Look what you can achieve with this little tiny\n\nCole Heilborn (23:27): Thing. I feel like I could go run a marathon with it. Right,\n\nBrian Seales (23:29): Exactly. And it actually encourages people to do it too. So it's a life changing device. If they just said, here's a wristband that counts your steps.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:39): I mean, I can get that in a cereal box.\n\nBrian Seales (23:41): You probably can. And it's like, will that be as effective as showing the lifestyle side of things?\n\nCole Heilborn (23:46): Yeah.\n\nBrian Seales (23:47): Fitbit's a good example of a really successful brand too.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:49): Yeah. Interesting. Well, so if people wanted to find you guys, where would they go?", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:07", "end_timestamp": "23:49", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "ba77c73c-c4fe-4338-b7af-4e82de77feae", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Brian Seales (23:54): Well, they can find us anywhere in Bellingham or everywhere. No, they can go to ketchup and mustard.com. They can just search ketchup and mustard in Bellingham or anywhere and find us our URL. You can imagine ketchup and mustard is not an unusual search term for people, although I don't know how many people are actually searching for it, but the domains to find it are already taken. ketchup-and-mustard.com. You can find us in Facebook and Instagram as well at ketchup and mustard design, and you can find us just above Taco Lobo in downtown Bellingham, at least between nine and five every day.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:31): Perfect. And how'd the name come about?\n\nBrian Seales (24:33): Well, so it's an interesting story. My wife and I started the business in 2010, and we wanted to create something, or I wanted to create something that was going to be memorable, a positive response.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:48): Tasty.", "token_estimate": 235, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:54", "end_timestamp": "24:48", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "cb1cb48c-be7d-4bb7-be0d-c0f7fcc34cd5", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Brian Seales (24:48): This is really, it's a branding tactic because our audience doesn't necessarily, we didn't think would necessarily gravitate towards ketchup and mustard the term, but we wanted something in the Pacific Northwest. It's really dark in the winter, so we didn't want to lean on the greens and blues that exist. We wanted a household term, something memorable that people already knew that didn't have to like, oh, I forgot what the name of this business is. And we kind of think of ourselves as being a really good addition to an existing organization. (25:17): So at the risk of calling all of our clients hot dogs or hamburgers, hot dogs and hamburgers are good, but they're better with ketchup and mustard in my opinion. So we become that portion of the project and we just make our companies, our clients a little bit better. It's not required, but it's just a little bit better. And there are all hot dogs. I think. I haven't determined if hamburgers or hot dogs are the right out there. I was a little concerned that it was a bit too playful For our core audience, we do a lot of stuff in medical and business to business, and there are some capital investment companies that we have and they're hiring like, oh, who are we hiring? Ketchup\n\nCole Heilborn (26:01): And mustard. Ketchup and mustard, the condiment\n\nBrian Seales (26:02): Suppliers. Right. And in certain situations, if we build a website for someone, we sometimes put built by ketchup and mustard. In some situations we just put built by k and m, so they don't really know.\n\nSpeaker 3 (26:12): Sure.\n\nBrian Seales (26:13): But a lot of people ask us the question, tell me more about ketchup and mustard. And then I get the opportunity to tell our story, which is a great icebreaker sales lead generator\n\nCole Heilborn (26:24): Develops rapport, develops some sort of relationship,\n\nBrian Seales (26:27): And then they know it. And then they go home and they see ketchup and mustard and they're like,\n\nCole Heilborn (26:31): They likely tell one of their friends about that agency that they met with.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "24:48", "end_timestamp": "26:31", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "2922592f-1588-4b14-8cf0-c416db87278a", "episode_id": "d1d3c836-dbf9-4d0c-a46d-20b17070181d", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4828", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Brian Seales (26:34): Yeah. And we're backing up our work really well too, so we're not the bang energy version of a design agency. We're actually backing. We do really good work, and you can see a lot of our stuff in our portfolio online if you are interested at all.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:47): Great. Well, thank you for your time today. I really appreciate it. But that's all of our time today. If you want to follow along, go ahead and hit the subscribe and like button and we'll see you next time. Sweet.", "token_estimate": 127, "speakers": ["Brian Seales", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:34", "end_timestamp": "26:47", "podcast_index": 1, "episode_name": "Ep 1: Brian Seales | Small Town Agency; Tasty Results | Ketchup and Mustard", "slug": "ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-1-brian-seales-small-town-agency-tasty-results-ketchup-and-mustard", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "In this podcast, I sit down with Brian Seales the Creative Director at Ketchup and Mustard and discuss how they as an agency approach solving their client's problems. It might surprise you, but the first step is often the hardest.", "podcast_length": "44:23"}, {"id": "487b4c72-baf7-4538-9bba-9fa37f306056", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Ian McWilliams (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling the fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Let's make waves. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. Today I'm sitting down with Ian McWilliams, the senior creative manager at Clean Canteen. Ian, how are you\n\nCole Heilborn (00:44): Doing great, Cole. Thanks for having me on.\n\nIan McWilliams (00:46): Absolutely. Thanks for joining us. So where are you at today?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:49): I am in South Lake Tahoe, California. So we are right here on the banks of Lake Tahoe in the heart of the Sierra Nevada Mountain Range.\n\nIan McWilliams (00:57): Awesome. So tell me a little bit about Clean Canteen.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:00): Yeah, so we are sort of a drinkware and hydration manufacturer, primarily positioned within the outdoor industry, but we also sell into a lot of other categories, kids home and gift, that kind of thing. The company is based here in California up north in a little town called Chico, sort of Northern Central Valley, tucked right up in the foothills there. Family owned business. Been around since 2004 and I think we're about a little over a hundred employees right now.\n\nIan McWilliams (01:31): Okay. I didn't know you guys were founded in 2004. I thought you'd been around for way longer.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:35): Yeah, 2004 was when the new ownership took possession of the business. Prior to that it had just sort of been in its infancy. So yeah, 2004 was really sort of when it turned into a brand.\n\nIan McWilliams (01:46): Okay, very cool. And what is your role with the company? What do you get to do?", "token_estimate": 523, "speakers": ["Ian McWilliams", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:46", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "aa80a5be-4f9c-4607-aacd-e0493715942d", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:51): Yeah, I'm the senior creative manager, as you mentioned, basically means I oversee our creative team here. We have a small crew in-house in Chico, and then we work with some outside agencies as well. So I get the fun job of overseeing every piece of creative that our consumers and dealers see. So everything from product copy to assets on the website to films that we produce to photography. So it's pretty multifaceted job and every day is completely different, especially in this day and age. Visual communications are evolving so rapidly and lots of new challenges, so it, it's a lot of fun.\n\nIan McWilliams (02:29): So you get to do all the fun stuff, it sounds like.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:31): Yeah, more or less. I mean, there's some less glamorous pieces of it as well, but yeah, we like to think it's all the fun stuff.\n\nIan McWilliams (02:38): So we've got a couple things I want to talk about today, but before I know you guys just launched your latest special project bottle, I'd love for you to fill us in. I think it's a really cool example of the creative that you are working on over there.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:51): Yeah, absolutely. Thank you for that. So yeah, we just launched a limited edition bottle that we're calling the Warming Stripes bottle. It's actually sold out. It's sold really well. So it's kind of said and done at this point, but it was basically highlighting a data visualization graphic done by this gentleman named Ed Hawkins, who's a pretty renowned British climate scientist. So the graphic basically was a series of stripes going from blue to red that basically represented our warming climate over the past, I think it was 160 some odd years. So it really shows the climate crisis that we're in a very visual way. So we kind of put this on a bottle as a way to create a talking piece for people that use this product to just think about actions that individuals can take in regards to climate action and sort of combating where we are right now with climate change. So yeah, it's a fun little project.", "token_estimate": 516, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian McWilliams"], "start_timestamp": "01:51", "end_timestamp": "02:51", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "8b65d61d-ffba-4ae1-beda-08e62456c42c", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Ian McWilliams (03:46): Yeah, I think that's a cool example of trying to break down a huge concept into something like tangible that could fit in your hands.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:52): Yeah,\n\nIan McWilliams (03:52): I think that's a great way to try to at least start the conversation.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:56): Yeah,\n\nIan McWilliams (03:56): For sure. Rather than diving all in, you start with something small.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:00): Absolutely.\n\nIan McWilliams (04:01): So I think our topic for today is authenticity, and I think it's a super interesting topic. It's obviously super relevant. It's everywhere in today's marketing world, and I'm hoping that we can kind of talk through this idea and I'd love for you to share your perspective on what authenticity is in marketing. And so maybe before we jump into some questions, I'd love to hear what your definition of authenticity is in the marketing world.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:29): Absolutely. Yeah, I think at its most basic, it's sort of a faithful and realistic telling of a story and sort of a larger concept beyond a product. I think specifically in consumer goods marketing within the outdoor world, that authenticity sort of takes on the recounting of a brand story or sort of a brand mission, and that's sort of how I see it in terms of how that concept flows into content creation.\n\nIan McWilliams (04:57): Okay. Yeah, that's really well said. Why do you think we're seeing such a big push for authenticity? It's become a buzzword now.", "token_estimate": 374, "speakers": ["Ian McWilliams", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:46", "end_timestamp": "04:57", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "fe3f355f-bf95-4336-90f2-8d917c84d659", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:04): Yeah, it really has. It's interesting and it's sort of unfortunate that it's become a buzzword because I truly believe it's such a core concept to the brand marketing that we do, especially in the outdoor space. I think why it's become so prevalent, I think content in general has really clearly ramped up in the past, I don't know, five to 10 years, something in that range on track with how social media has become more prevalent in our lives. I think it's just easier to absorb content. We just have content in front of our faces all the time. So I think there's literally more out there. So I think consumers and viewers of content are just getting more picky with the type of content they want to engage with, and I think that really brings it back around to content that tells an authentic story is generally from what I'm seeing, being the most engaged with content.\n\nIan McWilliams (05:57): Yeah. What indicators are you seeing or what do you have experience with?", "token_estimate": 249, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian McWilliams"], "start_timestamp": "05:04", "end_timestamp": "05:57", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "36995a43-f554-4454-95a4-5ea9a449a613", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (06:01): Yeah, absolutely. I mean, I think what we're seeing at Clean and other brands, I've worked for content that isn't so product forward that it's sort of all about that specific item is really doing well and being engaged with, I think telling sort of a story of what is your customer, your demographic look like when they're engaging with your product, how does that product fit into their life? The whole influencer marketing piece is sort of something that's muddied the waters in this a little bit. We sort of at Clean engage with that in quite a big way, but we kind of prefer to look at it as sort of ambassadors versus influencers. I think influencers in some ways almost become a little bit of a dirty word. I think we like to really try to create relationships with the people that were having create content for us and make it more than a transaction, and we like to work with people that are already familiar with the brand and have this product in their life already for the reasons that we like to talk about. Mainly sort of offsetting single use plastic and sort of replacing a plastic straw or a plastic coffee cup every morning with a reusable product that's going to be in service for years. So a little bit departure from your question, but I think that's sort of where my head's at on that.\n\nIan McWilliams (07:25): Yeah, interesting. So I feel like with everything, an idea becomes a practice, and then there's a bunch of Me Too practices outside of that, and I think maybe that's where we're at with the authenticity place, and I think maybe that's why it's become a buzzword and it's become kind of a taboo, not taboo, but it's like, oh, you're being authentic.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:47): Well,\n\nIan McWilliams (07:48): Everyone is.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:49): Oh\n\nIan McWilliams (07:49): Yeah. I guess what are some of the differences between brands and companies that are doing it really well and some ones that are just doing it because it's the thing to do?", "token_estimate": 499, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian McWilliams"], "start_timestamp": "06:01", "end_timestamp": "07:49", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "0690d189-d27d-409d-b7bc-8e983457fdb2", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (07:58): Yeah, absolutely. It's a great question. So I think one of the problems with that word authenticity is it's frequently used sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction. So like, Hey, Mr. Photographer, we need this image set to look authentic. So what does that mean to me? There has to be something behind that in order for it to truly be authentic. So I think the difference between a Me Too and a brand that really does have a story to tell is exactly that story piece. Clean Canteen for example, has the family owned side of things as well as all the third party certifications that we use to keep ourselves on track with our brand mission. So we're 1% for the planet member, which means we take 1% of our annual profits and those go to various charities and nonprofits that work within our areas of focus. (08:59): And then we're also a certified B Corp. So basically that means we meet high standards of social and environmental transparency and performance. So it kind of touches everything from how our supply chains are managed to how our employees in house are treated and that kind of thing. So I think a story like that is sort of the root of authenticity, and I think without that piece, you can't create content as a brand that really truly is authentic. Then I think also there's the product side of things. So a lot of the brands that I've worked for have sort of existed in a very specialized niche. So I worked for a company called Mont Bell early on in my career, and they're a manufacturer in the ultralight backpacking space. So they create ultralight jackets, ultralight shelters, that kind of thing. So very specific products that are filling a specific use that are designed by people that have a very specialized knowledge in that world that's kind of driven by experience. So I think that kind of concept versus a brand that is just coming in and saying, Hey, here's another object that may look cool but doesn't really have this use knowledge behind it or this sort of business for good piece to it, I think that's where things become problematic.", "token_estimate": 529, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:58", "end_timestamp": "07:58", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "ca5f5d9d-56e5-4f19-8f7a-100e215eab50", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Ian McWilliams (10:20): I see. So it is kind of the disconnect between why the company exists and what the messaging is. I feel like that's when consumers can really sniff out the fake brands versus the authentic ones.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:32): Exactly. Yeah, and it's interesting in the hydration world too. I mean, it's a little bit different than some other products because of the nature of a water bottle specific. There's not a whole lot of technology that goes into these things. I mean is some, don't get me wrong, but it's very easy for someone to say, Hey, I want to start a water bottle brand. They can go to 10 different factories that have this water bottle already manufactured and say, Hey, let's slap our logo on it. So I think that's something that the consumer may not necessarily see, but there's a huge difference between a water bottle like that and a water bottle that one of the other leading brands like Clean in this space makes is that there's just so much that goes into the design, the materials that are on the product, everything from the steel to the paint in terms of safety for the end consumer, as well as in terms of how the product is designed to meet specific use cases. And that's something you don't get with just picking a non-custom product off of a factory shelf.\n\nIan McWilliams (11:32): Sure, yeah. Why do you think we're seeing such an increase in the customer's desire to search out the why and the backstory of the brand rather than just choosing to make a choice based entirely on product feature?", "token_estimate": 386, "speakers": ["Ian McWilliams", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:20", "end_timestamp": "11:32", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "bcb63608-fbaa-40da-8f1f-53b3a0b0a544", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:45): Yeah, no, that's a huge question. It's a good one. I think it's really a broad thing. There's so much going on in the world, and I think that to some extent consumers feel empowered when they're able to make their dollars count when it's sort of an easy way, something that they're going to buy anyway could potentially contribute to a larger good, and I think more so now than ever just because of various things that are going on in our world. I think that's becoming more important to consumers buying decisions go beyond just the product and more into how can I make my $20 that I spend on a water bottle actually go farther than just that water bottle\n\nIan McWilliams (12:27): In your job? How do you make sure that what you guys are doing stays true to who you are? Because I think part of authenticity is a brand says something that doesn't align with really who the customer thinks they are. How do you make sure that you stay within those guidelines, I guess?\n\nCole Heilborn (12:44): Yeah, absolutely. I think it cleans specifically. That piece has been extremely easy for me, and I think a lot of our team would echo that as well. And I think that's simply because from the start, clean has been 100% unwavering on their mission. It really hasn't changed. I think they're so focused on creating products that are solutions to basically eliminate single use plastic waste, and it's as simple as that. That's sort of the premise, and then everything else within the brand just sort of sits beneath that. I think it's a pretty simple concept and it's been unwavering, so it's been fairly easy to keep on that path.\n\nIan McWilliams (13:21): Okay. No, that's well said. So if you had to give the industry other ways to describe authenticity, what are some other words that we could use so we don't beat a dead horse using that same word over and over?", "token_estimate": 470, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian McWilliams"], "start_timestamp": "11:45", "end_timestamp": "13:21", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "07c1aef6-e18d-4475-afdb-f7b547ad0dd4", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:33): Oh man. Yeah, that's a good question. Synonyms for authenticity within the outdoor world. I don't know, man, that's like an SAT question I think mean for me, true, accurate. I think true is a big one, true to the roots of the story that you're telling. I think it really just comes back to depicting what's really happening within a company. I think eliminating the smoke and mirrors and sort of putting yourself there with the end consumer is just so crucial in brand marketing these days. I think there's been really sort of a shift in eighties marketing where you have your spokes model standing next to the car saying, drink X, Y, Z drink. It's great. So we pivoted clearly more towards being there with the consumer and sort of pulling back that curtain a little bit and saying, Hey, we're out here on this photo shoot because we want to create these images that show you how our product works, and that's in order to give you an idea of whether this is something you want to buy, and then here's sort of a video to go along with that to show you how that product may fit into your life. (14:45): And that video is actually showing our employees or showing our friends or showing this ambassador that we've had a partnership with for 15 years. So I think it's just gotten much more real. Real is another good word.\n\nIan McWilliams (15:00): Yeah, there you go. Yeah, I think that's interesting. Touching on the photo and video for people who have been on a set, there's nothing real about a photo or a video set, and so in the goal to make something authentic, you're using practices that aren't authentic in order to create an authentic\n\nCole Heilborn (15:17): Outcome.\n\nIan McWilliams (15:19): It's just kind of ironic.", "token_estimate": 437, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian McWilliams"], "start_timestamp": "13:33", "end_timestamp": "15:19", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "e86512dc-4835-413c-b7fe-7cf0a168a389", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:20): It is ironic for sure, and we really strive, and I have in all the projects I touched to really almost take a more of a documentary approach. And to your point, there's inevitably stuff has to be set up. That's the nature of showing a product in use. But I think the more that you can sort of pivot away from that and set yourself up for showing real events, we've done photo shoots, a bouldering setup, for example, and it's crucial for us to find models and participants that are actually climbers and are doing that anyway and just sort of tag along and really sort of capture that moment more in an editorial sense than sort of a polished brand marketing sense.\n\nIan McWilliams (16:02): Yeah. So if you had to think about the future or a year or two years from now, what will this authenticity drive continue to look like in the future? Do we just see more of it? Do we see, I guess, what do we see?\n\nCole Heilborn (16:17): Yeah. No, I think it's going to become more and more important. I mean, I see these mission driven brands continuing to rise to the top of the pile. We've already sort of seen that to a pretty large extent in the last few years. I think that's only going to amplify, and I think that's just going to increase the need for this authentic content, and I think the consumer is going to even demand that more so than they have in the past. I think it's on the technical side of things like content creation has become, I don't want to say easier than it ever has been because it's an incredible art and skill, but it's more accessible. I think equipment is cheaper. There's more people out there doing it. The whole back to the influencer piece, there's people who are legitimately making a living as sort of a brand ambassador or influencer. So I think that whole sector is going to ramp up and I see things only getting bigger in terms of demand for that type of content.\n\nIan McWilliams (17:14): So looking at that then, how do you guys react to that idea or that future?", "token_estimate": 506, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian McWilliams"], "start_timestamp": "15:20", "end_timestamp": "17:14", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "2ea38536-2b39-4916-909e-382a94dc608a", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:20): I mean, I think it is just a continued investment in content and the time needed to create it as that's a hugely sort of time and cost intensive endeavor in a lot of ways. So I think there's challenges and sort of assigning ROI to some of that type of content, but there's also very clear cut cases of that type of content working. So I see in my world, I mean continuing to be a proponent of very sort of editorial feeling, real content and pushing for time and resources to do that. And then I think the other challenge is it really is on brand creatives to really help communicate and monetize why that content works and why it sells product. I mean it or not, at the end of the day, a lot of us are very involved in selling product, and that's a huge part of our jobs, and we have to justify the creative we make in that respect. So big challenge, but I think it's important and drives things in the right direction.\n\nIan McWilliams (18:23): Yeah. Well, you can't have a strong brand or creative without any sales.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:28): Exactly. Yep.\n\nIan McWilliams (18:31): Someone's got to pay for all the fun stuff.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:33): Yep. That's for sure.\n\nIan McWilliams (18:35): So tell me a little bit about your background, your journey looked like to get here.", "token_estimate": 326, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian McWilliams"], "start_timestamp": "17:20", "end_timestamp": "18:35", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "00c03345-8604-4a16-a4e6-769d0b5ae3a9", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:40): Yeah, absolutely. It's been a long one. I've been in the outdoor industry basically my entire career. I grew up in San Francisco, so in the middle of a big city, kind of counterintuitive to getting into the outdoor world, but I was kind of raised camping and being outside every weekend pretty much as a kid. So that sort of naturally drove me into outdoor retail pretty early on in high school and then through college, just had a huge interest in the activities and the gear that goes along with it and sort of how people interact with that gear. So one of my first jobs in the industry was working for a small mom and pop gear shop in downtown San Francisco right on Market Street, no longer around unfortunately, but worked there for quite a few years and have some fond and not so fond memories of cleaning out rental tents and doing the grunt work and never really imagined that that would take me to the brand side of things and didn't really have that foresight that that was an option when I was doing that. (19:37): But after college and getting an education in sort of film production and content creation, I was able to land my first job on the brand side of things out in Boulder, Colorado for Mont Bell, the company I mentioned earlier. And that was actually a really cool experience. We were, I think five people the biggest, so it was very bare bones crew. They're actually a subsidiary of a larger Japanese parent company at the time in the us. So we were just sort of that arm of the business. And being that there are five people, my job basically touched everything. So I was plugged into sort of designing the dealer workbook because I had some skillset there. And then my day would look like, oh, the phone would ring. I'd take that dealer workbook and reference it while I was selling the product to a dealer on the phone, and then go out to the warehouse and package that order and ship it off, and then a couple months down the road, go to a retailer and talk to that dealer in person. (20:33): So it was a really cool sort of end to end experience and something that I didn't realize how unique it truly was at the time, just because I didn't have that perspective. But looking back on it, that was such a key foundation to really help me be able to and sort of move through the industry as I grew. I think having a strong mentor in that first role was crucial as well. So that kind of drove me to more on the content marketing side of things as I progressed my career and worked for a little company called Teco Socks out in Boulder. They were a premium manufacturer of organic Marino, wool socks, ski socks, hiking socks, that kind of thing. So very mission-driven, a lot of story into the material sourcing and that kind of thing. So that was kind of my first taste at looking at these really complex supply chains and sort of big value propositions to the end consumer that were very complicated to communicate. (21:27): And that's sort of a thread that continued as I moved on to rough wear, manufacturer of technical dog gear of all things, great company out of Bend, Oregon. So yeah, we're a similar role there, sort of visual communications focus in the marketing world, and then moved on to C of Summit after that, another technical retailer in Boulder, similar type of role there. And all those roles. I got more and more specialized sort of on the creative design side of things and sort of the strategy behind delivering that content to the end consumer. And then that took me on to Clean Canteen where I've been for about three and a half years now, sort of running that piece of the business. So it's been cool. Then I just sort of dabble in freelance projects that come my way sort of in the creative space and mostly photography on the side of that. It's been a ride.", "token_estimate": 958, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:40", "end_timestamp": "18:40", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "33d320c0-b7bb-4631-aa9c-69b794874001", "episode_id": "514a647e-cccb-4e5c-87a2-92e875e02c88", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4837", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Ian McWilliams (22:19): No, that's great. And thinking about authenticity, I think so much of how we portray that comes from the experiences that you have, and so a more diverse background can often mean your ability as a marketer, I think, to communicate.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:33): Yeah, absolutely.\n\nIan McWilliams (22:34): Cool. Well, Ian, we're about out of time for today, but if people want to follow along with either a brand or with you personally, where can they find you?\n\nCole Heilborn (22:43): Let's see. Probably the biggest way to find me is ian mcwilliams.com, and then on Instagram I'm at Hey, duke, 1 9 8 2. And then Clean Canteen, of course is clean canteen.com.\n\nIan McWilliams (22:56): Perfect. Are there any more limited edition bottles that are coming out that we can know about?\n\nCole Heilborn (23:01): Well, not that you can know about, but we've got some stuff in the works, so definitely keep an eye out later this year. We're going to have some fun stuff.\n\nIan McWilliams (23:07): Sounds good. Well, Ian, thank you so much. Have a good rest of your day.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:10): Absolutely. Thank you, Coles.\n\nIan McWilliams (23:11): Good time. Take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 348, "speakers": ["Ian McWilliams", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:19", "end_timestamp": "23:11", "podcast_index": 22, "episode_name": "Ep 22: Ian McWilliams | Authenticity - It's Everywhere | Klean Kantee‪n‬", "slug": "ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-22-ian-mcwilliams-authenticity-its-everywhere-klean-kanteen", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"One of the problems with the word authentic is it's frequently used, sort of as an adjective within a piece of creative direction.\"", "podcast_length": "44:44"}, {"id": "96de41e8-6822-45b0-bf95-be127444fc4c", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Ian Mathias (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling the fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Porch Line Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Let's make waves. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. Today I'm sitting down with Ian Mathias, the director of Marketing Creative over at Strava. Ian, how are you?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:46): I'm good. It's a pleasure to talk to you.\n\nIan Mathias (00:49): Yeah. Where are you joining us from today?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:51): My home office, like everyone else in San Francisco. In Richmond, San Francisco. Yeah, just chopping wood at home.\n\nIan Mathias (01:01): I like it. So tell us a little bit about Strava. What do you do?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:04): Strava is the number one app for runners and cyclists. We are a social network training log and a tracking app for people who love to run and ride. So basically you can record your run or your ride with Strava. Then afterward, kind of geek out on the details, analyze your ride, compete against your friends, and then also the way that we are, whereas social networks, so you can post into the feed with your activity and then check out your friends' rides, your friends' runs, and leave comments, stuff like that.\n\nIan Mathias (01:38): Make sure your friends aren't beating you in those segments or taking those kms. Right?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:43): Yeah. If that's how you want to use Java, that's great. It's different for everybody, but yeah, a lot of people do love to compete with each other on segments around town.\n\nIan Mathias (01:52): Yeah, no, it's great. The app is used worldwide, right? I mean, I think almost everyone I know who runs recycles has used it or does use it.", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Ian Mathias", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:52", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "e267e4ec-2ead-4cdf-8608-27f4069c457b", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (02:00): I'm glad to hear that. Thank you. Yes, we've been very fortunate and grown a lot, especially in the last five years. We have about 60 million athletes all over the world. The US is our largest market, but actually about 80% of our athletes are outside of the us, particularly concentrated in Western Europe, especially the uk, and also a really, really big community down in Brazil. But yeah, we're everywhere. Very lucky to say, and it's just been such a pleasure and a privilege to get to work with athletes all over the world.\n\nIan Mathias (02:36): Yeah. How do you get to do that in your job? What do you get to work with?\n\nCole Heilborn (02:40): I'm responsible for the marketing team's creative output. I get to work with a bunch of amazing writers, designers, producers, marketers to basically make our marketing and help shape our brand. So as it pertains to getting to interface with our athletes, whenever we have videos to shoot, whenever we have campaigns to make, we really want to be representative not only of the athletes that are on the platform, but the athletes that we want to be on the platform the way we wish sport looks. So we're always looking to meet diverse, interesting, out there people, and that has taken me on shoots all over the world. And also just the beauty of the internet. You can talk to someone pretty much anywhere whenever you want. So yeah, that global nature has just made this a really particularly fun job and has really stretched my understanding of what it means to be a cyclist, what it means to be a runner or an athlete in general. It's different no matter where you are,\n\nIan Mathias (03:38): And I think that's cool too. You probably have a pretty unique perspective on athletes worldwide and through cultures and language, people everywhere run or if they have a bike cycle and they're kind of these cross culture methods of, I guess, experience that everyone gets to partake in. That's got to be really cool.", "token_estimate": 497, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Mathias"], "start_timestamp": "02:00", "end_timestamp": "03:38", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "beaea1b8-7131-4af9-830f-62485e58ea23", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (03:56): Oh, yeah, yeah. Sport is such a reflection on culture and the things that make your culture unique. Just find a way to come through and how you run ride. If you live in a place that is very safe, you're going to operate very differently than if you lived in a place that felt dangerous or if you're a member of a cultural community that is free to do whatever they want, you operate very differently than one that isn't. And there's just all the kinds of funds, societal norms as a society, very social or more kind of individualistic, and that shows up in how people run and ride. Oh yeah. We can talk about that for a long time, but it's just really interesting to see culture come through in the way people play.\n\nIan Mathias (04:48): Yeah. Well, that's another conversation again for another podcast episode, but where I'm hoping we can take today is talking about social media because I think, and this is something that on our phone call a couple of weeks ago, I think you helped me realize that Strava also is a social media network. It's just not maybe a conventional one based off of some of the other giants that are out there. And so I'm curious because I think social media is a really interesting topic. It's always, especially lately has been a hot topic in the news and it's very relevant. And so I'm curious if you could maybe give us a timeline of social media, an idea of where we've been, and then looking forward, where do you think it's going based off of some of the trends that we're seeing today?", "token_estimate": 386, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Mathias"], "start_timestamp": "03:56", "end_timestamp": "04:48", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "5de7cc55-bdda-4551-b462-8e472fe6c648", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:33): Sure. Yeah, I'll do my best to speak for the entirety of social media. I'm not working at a social network myself, but there's certainly all different kinds, and I think it is fair to classify Strava as one if the very basic material parts of a social network are a feed where you can post comment, give, like stuff like that, and that's certainly a part of the Strava experience. I think what makes us different is two things. One, I think that Strava is a participant network. And what I mean by that is if you want to be active on Strava, you kind of have to post content. And that's meaningfully different from a lot of other social networks. Most networks actually have users that are far more interested in watching other or looking, consuming other content than making content themselves. It's kind of like a lurker or a voyeur network. (06:29): And the vast majority of situations like on Instagram, Facebook, Twitter, all that, and I'm not here to say that's bad, but I have noticed an important difference with what happens on Strava is basically the way that you post on Strava is to upload a run ride. The activity is the central way to participate on our network. And in doing so, I really believe that it creates a sense of shared vulnerability that is missing in other networks. If you follow me on Strava, you are going to see more than just the destination of wherever I'm going. You're going to see the journey, you're going to see the ride I did the other day where I was kind of hungover and feeling like total crap and the run where actually I had to stop in the middle and find a bathroom or just those days that are just normal, just devastatingly normal. (07:27): And we all share that together. We all kind of upload these runs and rides together. And in doing so, I think it just chills everybody out that how can I have anything to say about your pace if the day before I posted a run that was even slower, and conversely, I can really share the excitement of seeing you finish your first marathon or seeing you win your first race on your bike or whatever experience that you get excited about. I can just so relate because I've posted that too and I know what it means, and I saw the eight weeks of suffering and work and grind that you put in place to get ready for that race or whatever. Whereas I think the same version of that experience on Instagram is just the picture with the metal around your neck at the end. So I think that's part of it is that when we have networks where people have to play, where you have to show up and you have to say, this is me and I'm trying my best and maybe I suck, but at least I'm brave enough to put it out there. (08:32): I think that really matters. And I think it affects the nature of the network and how people interact. And what we've noticed on Strava is a far more positive motivating kind of sense of belonging among the people in the community who use it and way less curation, hatred, trolling, and I think that's a big part of it. The other aspect I'd say that is really important to us being different and I think is very promising in this world of social media is Strava is a vertical social network. And what I mean by that is it's related to a niche interest. It's all about running and writing. And I've come to believe, and I don't think I'm alone in this belief, that one network might not be able to capture everything. Imagine being invited to a party. That's everyone you've ever met, and you can talk about anything you want. (09:36): It's just too much. It's too big of a container to fit in all these different things and ideas and kinds of people. And we're increasingly seeing people self-select into groups rooted in their interests around things that they want to talk about. And in doing so, it gives them permission to just nerd out and get really, really geeky about if I post a picture of my new bike on Instagram and a picture of my new bike on Strava, I expected people on Strava to be grilling me about my stem height and my gearing selection and what kind of bike is it? They're just going to give me questions and I can really go deep, and it's just a whole different environment. If I'm sharing that with everyone I've ever met on Facebook, I don't expect the same type of interaction. And I think that's not unique to this Java. There's a lot of other vertical networks that are doing this and finding a lot of success, and I could definitely see that becoming the future of networking and how we use the internet just increasingly focused on interest areas and giving ourselves permission to just talk about one thing and leaving the space for those other things for another time versus trying to cover it all at once all in one feed. I think that's really hard on people and not what the makers of those networks intended when they first started.", "token_estimate": 1221, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:33", "end_timestamp": "05:33", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "c25ece43-1d9a-447d-a8f1-ef617c4f526d", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Ian Mathias (10:56): Yeah. Why do you think that is? Why are we seeing a push away from those centralized feeds and towards these more hyper-focused feeds like Strava?\n\nCole Heilborn (11:06): Yeah, it's hard for me to speak for everyone, but I can easily speak for myself, and I have a real hard time with the context switching of the mass social network where if your feed is anything like mine, it's very difficult, especially lately where the first thing I see in my feed, just as an example, it might be related to the COVID death count, and that's just incredibly hard to process. And I'm one swipe away from seeing a baby photo that from someone I haven't met or I haven't even seen in years, I might even know them, and the next photo or the next thing I see has something to do with the Black Lives Matter movement, which is just so heartbreaking and important, and the next thing I see is somebody's sourdough recipe. It's just too much. I think that the human brain might not be cut out for that type of really heavy context switching where it's just so hard to know what are we talking about and do I like it all the same? (12:06): Do I like that sourdough recipe or the picture of your big bubbly crumb? Do I like that the same way, I like the same tool that I like this Black Lives Matter protest? It is just too much. And I think you would never conduct a conversation that way. You and I would never talk to each other that way. We would stay focused on a subject and gently move from one to the other. And I think that that, at least for me, that context switching and that free for all, that feeling that anything could happen at any moment, we can talk about anything here is just too much. And instead, what I want to do is focus on one thing at a time and have the freedom to go deep to really get into it and talk about the minutiae and feel like it's a safe space where I'm only talking about that.", "token_estimate": 480, "speakers": ["Ian Mathias", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:56", "end_timestamp": "11:06", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "c597385a-1382-404e-be2f-08a72f01f8dc", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Ian Mathias (12:53): So back to your first point, is it the platform? Is it the people that use it that are, when you post a photo on Instagram, it has to be absolutely perfect, but when you post something on Strava, for example, you post that picture with a snot rocket coming down your nose across your cheek and you don't really care, I guess, what is different about those two platforms that has such a difference in the content that's published?\n\nCole Heilborn (13:20): Yeah, I think it gets back to that notion I mentioned minute ago about the journey and destination. And in a way I could see how this also explains the success of Instagram stories and that what we were noticing on social, which has inspired a lot of the branding work we've done at Strava, is that desire to curate this instinctive, it's very unspoken, but everyone feels it that they should be presenting their best selves. And this is about celebrating your accomplishments and in a way even projecting what you wish you were to your very, very, very best moments that are actually really fleeting and rare. Whereas on Strava, the way to use this platform is to post your activity good or bad, and acknowledge that it is building up to something greater, that if you want to run a marathon, you have to run a lot of really short runs. (14:11): You have to have a lot of training, and everyone who's been there gets it that you just can't show up and do the thing that you want to do. So I think that emphasis on the journey really matters. It really matters that everyone knows that this is a step along the way and I'm trying, I'm doing my best and maybe this wasn't my best run, but it'll help me get to a better one. And can you imagine Instagram and Instagram feed like that? It'd be so cool. These were the hundred awful photos I took to get this one photo that looks really great and maybe have to swipe through. I think that'd be a really killer post. I mean, it would clog up the feed to be a mess, but you know what I mean? It's just so cool to see the journey and see the effort and through that, the vulnerability and the acknowledgement that, look, I'm just trying. Yeah, I think that really matters.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Ian Mathias", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:53", "end_timestamp": "13:20", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "671cb9ac-2069-4385-bbd7-58558ae1deba", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Ian Mathias (15:00): Yeah, no, that's really well said. And I think that's a lot of the problem that we have with social media is we see all of these summit moments and then you look at the amount that we have and we're like, well, shoot, I don't have nearly as many of those, but in reality, those are pretty far and few between for most people.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:19): Yeah, it's true. And I also think that a good network should have utility, and a big part of what Strava is up to and what we really hope to be for our athletes is just incredibly useful that it's not just about seeing the summit shot as it's a great example you just made, but also of like, Hey, this is the route to the summit and you can go do it too. Here's how I did it. Go try it yourself. And what we really want the feed to be for our athletes isn't this source of doom scrolling, just never ending, scrolling, scrolling, scrolling. What we want it to be is a source of motivation, a way to discover new places to play to see incredible achievements or really grindy workouts and get inspired by them and go do 'em yourself. So that's I think another aspect of these vertical networks. When you get really specific about a subject matter, you can then start to add utility and tools to make that subject matter come to life and just be more useful to your users as opposed to strictly about sharing content.\n\nIan Mathias (16:21): I\n\nCole Heilborn (16:21): Really hope that when people use Strava, they get a sense of motivation that they close their session ready for their next runner ride. That's like the dream. The last thing we want to do is have someone just feel like they wasted 30 minutes of their lives, they should be fired up and ready to go do an epic run or plan their next big ride, whatever. I think that's an important part too.", "token_estimate": 456, "speakers": ["Ian Mathias", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:00", "end_timestamp": "16:21", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "62a378c5-baaf-45fd-ac8a-e50a4a0404bf", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Ian Mathias (16:44): Yeah. So some of the things that I know and this speaking personally that I have problems with social media, there's two big things. One of them is just all the ads and all the stuff that you don't care about, but you're forced to look at. And then the second is the question of where is my data going and how is it being used and who is using it. Just recently you guys came out with a pretty big update slash PR or messaging, and I'm hoping we can dive down that and explain what you guys have decided to do. Where do you want to take the company and I guess explore the why behind that?", "token_estimate": 153, "speakers": ["Ian Mathias"], "start_timestamp": "16:44", "end_timestamp": "16:44", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "2375d918-c1bd-4500-b3a4-fbcc5bb6eb05", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (17:22): Sure, yeah, I'm happy to talk about it. So as some background, we came to a crossroads as a business probably like many people and many businesses did in 2020 and really had to decide what we wanted to be. And our founders came back to lead the company and made it very clear that we are going to dedicate ourselves to our community, especially our subscribers. Strava is unique among networks that we have a paid offering, it is free to use, but you can pay to get our best features. And we felt that now is the time to invest in those who have invested in us and to build the best subscription service for athletes in the world. What that meant very practically is we added a bunch of free features that were outstanding requests. We really tried to get better and continue to try to be better at listening for what our athletes are asking for. (18:16): We also ripped out a lot of aspects of Strava that didn't feel like they were serving the athlete, that they were serving a different kind of customer. So for example, we had a algorithmic feed. So when you looked at Strava, the feed was sorted in a way that we thought you would've liked the most. A lot of our athletes did not like that, so we switched it back, even though they may or may not be able to find what they're looking for easier, just that logical ordering worked better, so we wanted to give them that. Another example is we took out sponsored integrations, which is a feature that is really the closest thing we've ever had to ads in the feed. We thought it just wasn't adding to the athlete experience, so we took it out. And then the last thing as part of this change was we added more value to our subscription. So we made some net new subscription features and we took a few free features that were really expensive to maintain and made them part of the subscription. (19:14): I can't get too much more into what it means for the business, but I can definitely talk about that, how it makes me very excited to be a marketer and build this kind of brand because this gives me such a more eye to eye relationship with our customer. It's simple and honest. Actually in a traditional way. I bet anyone listening who sells a normal product thinks this is sold fashioned and normal, but in software, it is not novel to make a product so good that people are happy to pay for it, and that's what we want. It's that simple, and that's a new model for networks. Many software companies put their focus on customers who are not their users. We don't want to turn people into a product to sell. We want to make an awesome product for awesome people to buy, and at the same time, we're going to keep making improvements to our free product for people who can't afford to subscribe or we just haven't convinced them yet that it's worth buying. (20:18): And that's such an exciting job as someone who's building a brand at a software business to just compete for that, to want to convince you of that as opposed to just finding a way to keep you using the platform. As I said a minute ago, we want nothing more than to be useful. If you subscribe to Strava and use it once a week and you're happy, I'm happy, but on many other networks that ain't, so once a week wouldn't be enough. We want to keep you back, keep you coming back, keep you using so that we can serve you more ads. And that is just so plainly not our business model now, and I think that creates a very exciting opportunity for us as a brand, but also as a business. And I hope that this is one of the revelations of a society that we have over the next five to 10 years. Our relationship with technology is changing. I think we're past that period of irrational exuberance where every app should be free and using free technology has no consequence. We saw that in media five, 10 years ago, and I bet it happens soon enough with technology too.", "token_estimate": 975, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:22", "end_timestamp": "17:22", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "66699d20-5cb6-4fa7-97fd-f99e86f7a762", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Ian Mathias (21:43): Well, if an app is free, the company is making money and they're making money in other ways, and oftentimes that's ads. And I think now more than ever, people are dissatisfied with ads. They're obtrusive. And when you're spending time on your feed and you want to look at that photo of your new grandchild or your friend's baby, and then you see an ad for, I dunno, spark plugs, it takes away from that experience. And so dive a little further into why you guys decided to remove the sponsored element from Strava.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:18): Yeah, I think you just articulated it so well. It's about experience and we are not yet a profitable company, so we're in a great place where we're able to decide how do we want to profit, how do we want to become a company that is profitable? And it's so much more desirable for us and for our customer to invest in their experience and making an experience so good that they want to buy it. It's just such a natural relationship to have with the customer. And we are also not a huge company. We're very flattered by how people think. We're a big company and we're so lucky to have so many athletes on the platform all over the world, but we only have 180 employees and we can only have them do so much, and they're more motivated and our customers are more motivated by having them focus on making awesome experiences for athletes. And making an ads business is not just a switch you flick on. It's something that you spend a lot of time to do, and it's just so much more pure to go after making that experience. Great. And I think that's what we want to be.\n\nIan Mathias (23:35): Yeah. So from your perspective in the marketing department, you get to take all of this and figure out how to talk about it. What is that like, and what are some projects that you have worked on that you're really excited about, or what are some things that are coming up that you're working on if you can talk about 'em?", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Ian Mathias", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:43", "end_timestamp": "23:35", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "97b68565-91a6-4bd9-8d75-09a0b3157aa0", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:52): Sure. Yeah. Well, I mean, we talked a lot about this change with our athletes and we knew it would be hard. It's always a challenge to take some free features and make them paid, but I found it pretty easy to do as a marketer in so far as that. One of my favorite quotes is a twain quote. I'll paraphrase it, and it's tell the truth and you don't have to remember anything. And that's really our approach to making this change is if we're honest about what we're doing as a company and what we think it could become and how we really genuinely believe we're acting in the best interest of athletes. You don't have to spin it. It's not a big flashy campaign. You just tell the truth and then just keep believing and keep investing and making that experience great. And what we've been doing all year long and we'll continue to do is focus our marketing on how we are improving the experience of Strava, acknowledging that some people love us already and some people don't love us enough. (24:49): You have to subscribe and we need to earn their trust in their business. And I'm happy to try just to continually showcase how our product engineering and design teams are making great new features and helping them bring those features to market. It's kind of old school marketing, but I really appreciate it as a low flash, honest way of just grinding out like, we're going to make this thing better and better and better until you subscribe. And even if you don't subscribe, you can still use it for free. That's not a bad deal. So that's been a lot of the work lately. We've also done some brand work that I've really loved that's related to a lot of the things we talked about earlier. We had a campaign a couple of years ago called Athletes Unfiltered. It happened not long after the 2016 election where the idea of social media was really taking a nose dive very quickly, went from a good thing to a bad thing in the eyes of the world, and we really took issue with that generalization and for everything we talked about earlier about Strava being positive, motivating people, feeling that they belong on Strava, they don't have to curate their posts. (26:02): We made the simplest campaign. It was actually so simple as we were making it. I was worried it was too simple, which is probably a great sign that you're doing something great. We just went out with a video crew and asked our athletes how they felt about their sport and about Strava and made a bunch of vignettes and a hero film that showcased the difference between the experience on Strava and that of other networks. And it was probably our most successful campaign ever, just because I really think we were able to isolate that tension happening in the world and that Hal Strava was an antidote to it, which I think is always the basis for a good campaign insight.", "token_estimate": 714, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:52", "end_timestamp": "23:52", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "a6787a9f-be5d-44d9-b0c6-212bfd78ce8a", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Ian Mathias (26:44): Yeah, no, that's really well said. Where can people find those videos if they're interested in 'em?\n\nCole Heilborn (26:50): Our YouTube channel, you can definitely go back through and look at all of our campaigns over time, at least our video campaigns. Yeah, the YouTube channel would be a great place to start, and definitely you can follow us on other social networks or on the Strava Club. Actually, that's an interesting thing we've been doing lately too. We essentially made our version of at Instagram through our club's platform on Strava, where you can basically join the Strava Club and then whenever you want updates from Strava or highlights of cool things happening in the community, we'll post that in the feed and it'd be an amazing social experiment. If there's any sociologist out there who would like to look at this occasionally, we do post really similar content on Twitter, Facebook, Instagram, and the Strava Club. And the nature of the reactions and comments we get are so different on each platform in a way that, I dunno, I think would be very interesting to look deeper into it. Yeah, we've been doing a lot lately building this Strava Club up as a place to maintain that positivity, motivation, support inside our own wealth.\n\nIan Mathias (27:56): No, that's cool. So as you're looking forward into the future of Strava, what do you hope for? What do you hope the outcome of the app is for your athletes?", "token_estimate": 363, "speakers": ["Ian Mathias", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:44", "end_timestamp": "27:56", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "8115a7da-a4c3-4cd0-8a92-d5879593f83d", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:11): Well, a couple things. I hope that they find it incredibly useful and additive to their lives. The last thing that we want to make is technology that just keeps you occupied and that, like I said earlier, if you have a 10, 20, 30 minute session, you get to the end and wish you had that time back. That'd be such a shame. If nothing else, I hope our athletes use Strava and they put their app down and it just inspires 'em to go out. Actually, how fast can we get you off the platform and out there running and riding? That would be a great goal for us and something that I hope we just get better at, better at being useful. (28:51): The other, which is very related to the moment, is just increasingly diverse. Running and cycling are prevalent among mostly privileged white communities and affluent areas. Yet they are in many ways, very accessible sports that are experienced all around the world. And I'm really interested in building a world that we wish was how we understand sport versus one we have now and introducing all kinds of new people to the sport and bringing their cultures, their experiences into our sports in a way that makes the whole thing just even more fun and different. I think Strava is one of the few companies really positioned to do that because we have 60 million athletes already using it and from all over the world. I think it could be a really powerful force for unity in a time of division and diversity and inclusion in a time where that's really hard to come by too. So we've got our work cut out for us, but that's just so interesting too, and I think a place where we can make a difference.\n\nIan Mathias (29:57): Yeah. Well, that was beautiful. I think we're about out of time for today, but if people want to follow you, I mean, I know people can find Strava, but who want to find you, where can they follow your work", "token_estimate": 471, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Mathias"], "start_timestamp": "28:11", "end_timestamp": "29:57", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "9c2f2baa-c890-4852-8170-ebdf57318121", "episode_id": "436cf23a-043f-47f9-aa15-3709eb972729", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4838", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (30:10): On Strava? Of course. Well, I mean, joking aside, I, I'm an athlete. I love to run. I love to ride, and I welcome anyone to follow me on Strava. Always look for us on our other social channels, YouTube, Instagram, Facebook, Twitter. We love to invite new people to our community and share our work. But if there's only one place, come on Strava. Yeah, we'd love to see you there and I'll follow you back.\n\nIan Mathias (30:37): Perfect. Well, Ian, thank you so much. Have a good rest of your day and I hope you can get out on a ride or run or something this weekend.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:43): I hope so too. Thanks. It's been a pleasure.\n\nIan Mathias (30:46): Alright, take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 227, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Ian Mathias"], "start_timestamp": "30:10", "end_timestamp": "30:46", "podcast_index": 23, "episode_name": "Ep 23: Ian Mathias | Get Out Of My Feed | Strav‪a‬", "slug": "ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-23-ian-mathias-get-out-of-my-feed-strava", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "Embrace the sweat, love the failure, and keep going regardless if you reach the podium. Does this culture sound different than your average social network giant? Sounds kind of nice doesn't it?", "podcast_length": "44:45"}, {"id": "90816292-831f-4827-89e2-95e1a82c4cde", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling the fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Porch Line Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Let's make waves. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn, and today I'm sitting down with Patrick McCarthy, the team manager over at 6 8 6. Patrick, how are you?\n\nPatrick McCarthy (00:47): I'm doing fantastic. As fantastic as we can be doing during a global pandemic. And yeah, I mean, weather's starting to turn up in the northwest. We're getting away from the constant flow of heavy rain and starting to see some of that sunshine and making some dirt fly up on some trails on the mountain bike, and snow's pretty much dried up at this point, so we're moving into some other territory, but it's been good. I've been spending a ton of time with my family and hanging out in Bellingham, which is a beautiful place this time of year.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:25): It really is, and I'm stoked that you can join us. There's not a lot of people who have been on the podcast who are also from Bellingham, Chad, he was one of 'em, Bellingham. It's a cool place to be, but there's not a lot of people in the marketing world that live here.", "token_estimate": 413, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:25", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "85c6b2cb-abdd-40e2-8f71-d797c4c21eae", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Patrick McCarthy (01:39): Yeah, I feel very fortunate. 6, 8, 6 brought me on to do the team management and work in marketing for them, and being able to work remotely is key. It's key for them too, because they need to bring authenticity to creating outerwear and snowboard and being in the mountains. So them being based in Los Angeles, it's important to have a voice here in the mountains. They're constantly sending prototype jackets and new concepts and ideas up to me, and I can go out and wear it up at Baker and pound on it in the rain and in the conditions and actually get them the proper feedback. And a lot of the riders want to come through the area, and it's a good hub for them. So it's really nice. It's nice to, I always enjoy my time going to Los Angeles and working there too, and the juxtaposition from Bellingham to LA and everywhere in between. So yeah, I love it up here. Don't really want to move too badly. It's a really fun place to live and lots of cool outdoor activities, which is what I'm really into it is getting out, being in the snow, being in the mountains in any way, shape or form. And I mean, we have the islands here too, but yeah, I feel very fortunate to get a chance to work out of here, and I think it's a very creative, good space.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:00): Yeah. Well, tell us a little bit about six eight.", "token_estimate": 337, "speakers": ["Patrick McCarthy", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:39", "end_timestamp": "03:00", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "560af568-8cbc-427e-a2ba-e904c77cd2fc", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Patrick McCarthy (03:02): 6, 6, 8. Six is a global outerwear brand. I might get this number wrong, but 23 or 24 or 24 years independently owned outerwear company, creating everything from stuff for snowboarders, skiers. And we have other products now with this multi collection that we created where we're making things that are really good for going out climbing, going out hiking, going out, mountain biking, a lot of new offerings from the brand to diversify and grab a larger amount of the population. But you can find 6, 8, 6 anywhere from, we always encourage people to buy from your cool core shops in every area. There's dealers all over the world and reps all over the world. So we're selling in Germany and Austria over in Japan and Korea and China and all throughout the United States and Canada. And we make everything from super high-end Gore-Tex options that you can find at REI or down to really cool creative industry brands such as the Hundreds and Primitive Skate and make cool boutiquey stuff that they sell at core shops, or even at Zoomies is another big dealer of ours. So we try to make a little bit of outerwear and a little bit of our collection is for everybody from your beginner to the top level. So\n\nCole Heilborn (04:50): Yeah, anyone who's spent some time in the snowboard or ski world probably has run across your guys' products before.\n\nPatrick McCarthy (04:57): Like you\n\nCole Heilborn (04:57): Said, you've been around for a long time, but I'm really curious because you've got a really cool background and before you were working on the brand side, you were a rider, you've gone through the sponsorship, you've been through the whole ambassador stuff. So what's that? Tell us a little bit about your background, and I'm curious, what's it been like for you working on the brand side now for some time with your background out in the field?", "token_estimate": 469, "speakers": ["Patrick McCarthy", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:02", "end_timestamp": "04:57", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "ba92fbfc-deee-4234-8091-63dcbff4ac1b", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Patrick McCarthy (05:19): Basically, I was like every other human being. I love to go to the mountain, love to snowboard and skateboard and do whatever those things, but I also, I was worried about my future. So after high school, I got into Western Washington University and started at the college, and then going into my third year, I was offered the opportunity to drop out of school and become a professional snowboarder. That lasted for the greater part of 10 years, maybe a little bit longer than that, 10 to 12 years. And then after traveling and filming for various movie projects and doing various photo shoots and traveling all over the world and enjoying that lifestyle for a super long time, 6, 8 6 approached me and they asked me if I would be interested in helping manage their team, managing some photo shoots in the beginning, kind of becoming a team manager slash rider, and it grew from there and they asked me to take on more marketing roles as time goes on and continue to run that side of it. (06:27): So I think I always have known that everything that I learned along the way of being a snowboarder and dealing with the various team managers that I dealt with and working with all the different sponsors that I had, I learned a little bit and took a little bit away from every single experience that I had. And now, luckily enough, I'm doing the team managing and I'm able to share all the insight that I gained from working with 20 different sponsors over my career and helping share a little bit of the insight that I have. And I'm already in tune with all the filmers. I'm already in tune with all the photographers. For me. I have a lot of interpersonal relationships that I can pull from, and I have that mental Rolodex that I can go into and all the riders and I can always see things from team rider's perspectives. (07:22): I went through everything. I know when you're out there and you're filming for crazy movie project and you're just trying to get those last shots and you're running out of travel budget and sleeping in your car to save money and doing what you need to do to make it work when you're on the road just trying to get that last double or 10 80 or 900 or seven 20 or whatever it is that you want to really make yourself proud of what you're working on. I know what those riders are going through. So I try to share the insight and kind of be a liaison from the company to the riders and underst it. Now I can totally understand it from both sides and understand both sides perspectives. The company wants to continue to sell gear and be progressive and give back and do rad things, and the riders want to follow their dreams and push themselves and represent the brand. So it's like helping that become one cohesive working unit. That's my specialty.", "token_estimate": 700, "speakers": ["Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "05:19", "end_timestamp": "05:19", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "796af396-d08b-427c-9df9-5c10f4daac1f", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (08:27): Yeah. So from the brand side, because the world ambassadors have been around for a long time representing brands, and the word ambassador is almost kind of turned in, there's another form of it now, an influencer. It's a little bit different than an ambassador, certainly for 6 86. Why do you guys invest heavily into your ambassadors? What's the goal there?\n\nPatrick McCarthy (08:47): I think the goal there is finding these unique people from the different regions all over the world that have made a huge impact in their region that have some of the best, I feel like 6 86 has some of the best professional snowboarders and skiers in the world on our team. And from Gigge rough over in Europe, who's been one of the biggest Austrian snowboarders, bavar area like snowboarders in the world to Forrest Bailey and now Hanna Beman on our female side and Parker White on the ski side. These guys all bring a really insane dynamic element to the sport and make it cool in their own way. And I think the kids look up to them. They have big social media following, so they post stuff about the gear, they post stuff that they're hyped on. The kids love it, and it creates a following. (09:46): And I think it's really important for brands. I'm really proud of the way 6, 8 6 does this, and it's like a way to give back to the sport to support these taste makers and to make their lifestyle and to bring that to fruition. And yeah, I think you bring up a really good point. There is a whole movement of influencers and people that just have these crazy social media fallings, and they might not even necessarily have all that much skill, but they know how to post about it. So I think there's a place for that here and there, but that's not really my wheelhouse. And it is something that's growing and something that we're aware of, and it is starting to be like, oh, you want to get a product out, you send it to this guy, and he puts it up on social, and there's part of that, but I think it's so important still to support the real core snowboarders that are out there going for it and pushing the sport and progressively. (10:44): But what you got to do is look at what kind of demographic you're trying to sell to. 6, 8, 6 is not only selling to the top best kid at every resort they want to be for the beginner and for diversity, and for every element, we want to create something for everybody, whether you're going on a hike with your family or whether you're doing a front side 10 80 over the Mount Baker road gap, or whether you're competing in the Olympics doing 1440 bloody Dracula, or whether you're a rad chick from the Midwest that's having fun with our friends. We want to create something cool for everybody. So the influencers, there's a time and place for that. It's something that I have a hard time grappling with because just when people have a big following, I've been around a lot of these influencers and I'm just like, what do you do? (11:39): What do do with your time? Are you willing to sacrifice yourself for this? Because the snowboarders that I work with, they do stuff that's like do or die. If they don't make what they're trying to do, they could literally die and they're pushing themselves and they're just going off. And an influencer just is going on Instagram every two seconds. And a lot of the time when I've hung out with big influencers, they just sit there and post all day and they don't even live it. So that's what I feel. That's my own personal, that's not really necessarily maybe the brands we're all learning this new landscape, but that's just my own personal take on it.", "token_estimate": 911, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "08:27", "end_timestamp": "08:47", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "6fd0906b-128a-4ef9-a798-fa015655af5b", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:19): Yeah, no, that makes sense. I mean, you've been there, you've been in that world. So when you look at the ambassador program that you guys run, it's not something that it's not a small expense. There's a lot of time and a lot of resources that are put towards it. So when you guys look at quantifying, why should we keep investing in these riders, A lot of what you're saying it sounds like is you guys choose to do it because you want to invest in the sport, you want to invest in the people that are pushing it. So is that why you justify that expense, or do you also look at it and try to, I guess, what are the marketing tangibles that you try to extract from that as well?", "token_estimate": 175, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:19", "end_timestamp": "12:19", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "c78e3fdf-adaa-4863-8b87-080b9f8c486e", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Patrick McCarthy (12:56): The marketing tangibles that we're looking to extract out of having a team, and that is basically every season, we will create a line that will come out the next year. And so throughout the season, what I do is I'm traveling around with this rainbow set of the key items from our company that will come out the next year, and I'm putting those on the team riders and taking them out doing photo shoots. So we're shooting all this gear that comes out the next season in the current season. So when this next season starts right around those early preseason, beginning of December, we'll go out and we'll have all next year's gear and we'll start shooting the guys in it. They'll be hiking, they'll be hitting jumps, they'll be making turns, they'll be riding lines, they'll be ripping it. We'll be doing even just some lifestyle stuff. (13:53): But then they help us create the assets. And then also when they wear the gear, they say, oh, if this pocket was a little bit switched or a little tweak change, or maybe this pocket doesn't even need to exist, and this vent needs to come down a little bit more. And I don't understand with this hood, this hood needs more circumference. We need to have more room in there. So we get our feedback from them, we get our assets from them that we can utilize in the magazines that we market with or on our online social pushes. (14:32): And then not only do I have that Rambo set, but each one of them get certain key products that we want to focus on with each person, whether it's our street style riders that are hitting more urban stuff, or our big mountain skiers and our free ride tour riders will all be wearing the most bomber, Gore-Tex, our highest end pieces and out there in the field giving us feedback and representing the brand in those ways. So that's why it's so important to have those people because getting, bringing true authenticity to all the people that sell your product too. You're like, dude, look at this guy wears it and he swears by it, or this woman, she always wears this piece and this piece and this piece. And we try to hopefully tell that story down through our reps and so that they can pass that along to the retailers and we can tell the right stories and we can stand behind it and feel like we're proud of what we're doing.", "token_estimate": 583, "speakers": ["Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "12:56", "end_timestamp": "12:56", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "170b2401-fb87-473f-8c29-3a456b9e066f", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (15:39): So how has the ambassador programs changed since you were in it?\n\nPatrick McCarthy (15:43): I think it's changed a little bit over time. I think that there's more people out there doing it. There's more riders. There's probably more talent out there than there was before, and I think a larger range, but I don't think there's been that much that's changed about it. Social media in general, Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, all these new platforms that have come out have definitely made it different. When I was a pro snowboarder, you were going out with a photographer and he was shooting these photos on a slide, and the filmers were out there in the back country with 16 millimeter Bolex cameras and they're filming it on film. So you're out there and you're doing all these things. Then it's like, okay, you wait until September to fly to San Diego and they're going to do a huge movie premiere at the trade show, and you're going to be there for it, and then your photographers are going to submit their slides to transworld and to snowboarder into frequency, and you're going to wait and see if you got a few photos. (16:53): Well, dude, that has been the biggest shift in my opinion because it's like that's what it was like for me, and now it's today. It's like you go out, you get a few shots, maybe you're like, okay, that one's going up on Instagram. This one's going in my story. This one's going, I am going to use this one on this social media platform. I'm going to throw this. I'm going to save this for my little video project that I'm going to drop on Vimeo or whatever. And so there's just all these elements to it that have evolved so much and changed. So I think that that is just basically, that has made it quite a bit different.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:34): Does the social media element, does that change how your writers or you interact with the sport? Do you feel like that takes away from the sport at all?", "token_estimate": 488, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "15:39", "end_timestamp": "17:34", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "9b219c51-51c4-405d-8132-7c801d4b2f40", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Patrick McCarthy (17:42): I don't think it takes away from it. It makes it here now right in front of your face. It's like a lot of the times, one of the things that I find a little bit disheartening, but you can't get around it, is the whole concept of the viral video or whatever. Sometimes you can put up a stupid little thing from your phone that you didn't even think was that important, and it gets millions of views. And then you might make something in a really well-produced, amazing piece of digital cinematography and spend a ton of money, and that might get 3000 views because a kid won't sit for 12 minutes to actually watch something that's heavily produced like that, and maybe we can't market it to 'em properly. I have a really funny example. It's actually happened to me this last month. (18:38): I actually didn't even shoot it. So one of my team riders was following a group of us through this snake run, and it's like everyday thing and everyday thing. Anybody could go ride. It's on the normal run. You didn't put any extra effort in. It wasn't beautiful snow conditions. It was just five guys running through a little trail. And my buddy had filmed it and I saw it and I was like, I'm just going to post this. It's been the pandemic. Nothing, didn't think anything about it. So I posted it and people loved it. People were like, oh, I missed that. That's the element that I love. Well, then Snowboarder magazine DMed me and they're like, Hey, do you mind if we post this? And they posted it, and then when they posted it, they got 400,000 views on it, and then not only 400,000 views, but then it got 287 shares. (19:32): So what happens is there's all these, this is part of social media that drives me insane, but there's all these people that have board world and board great and board board all these different apps, and they just steal stuff from SNOWBOARDER Magazine and post it on there and they get like 20,000 followers, 30,000 followers. They don't do anything. They don't do any of the content generation. They aren't cool. They didn't go live it. They have no credibility, but they have groups of people that come in and follow them. They steal stuff from whatever they want. So in the end, dude, I think that this little video that I actually didn't even film, I was the dude in the front, one of my team writers film. They thought it was me. It all went through, it got posted on all these things. It got probably two or 3 million views and it was a pointless little moment. (20:30): And that's where it's changed. And so then you think about I go out and I'm pushing myself and I get an amazing shot that I'm so proud of, and that'll go up and it won't have that sort of viral element to it. So it's something that we don't even really have the control over. You don't have any control over it. You don't know what's going to go viral. I mean, obviously if a dude has a ton of followers, he's going to get big views or whatever, but there's no rhyme or reason to how it's working right now, and it's evolving so fast. It's like I have tons of my younger riders on the team now are all like, oh, you still post on Instagram? We're all on TikTok now. And they're getting millions of views on that. And so I don't know, it's a quickly evolving game, try to stay up to speed with it all, but also try to bring as much authentic things to it as you can, I guess.", "token_estimate": 849, "speakers": ["Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "17:42", "end_timestamp": "17:42", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "463f6cf5-86e9-48f6-a40e-1230fc4400ab", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (21:37): So knowing that, that there's this huge appetite for just small, random stuff, how do you guys plan out your media if the big, huge films are successful, but also just the random cell phone shots are also extremely successful? How do you guys plan out what you post and what you need?\n\nPatrick McCarthy (22:00): The way that we plan it out a lot of the times without giving too much away is we look at the line. What's important in our marketing is look, what do we do? We sell snowboard, ski and mountain culture outerwear. So we look at the line, we look at what pieces are important to us, what our key pieces are. We go out, we get the assets, and then we create a battle plan of how we want to drop the assets. The following year. We are like, let's focus this week on the smarty technology. Let's focus this week on the new Gore-Tex offerings. Let's focus this week on the women and what they're doing. Let's focus on this rider. Let's focus on this product. And we base it around the products. I think a lot more now. And then also, it's like another way you could look at it is like, let's say we come out with this thing and we're just really excited about it. (22:59): Well, what if everybody's really excited about it and then all of a sudden you realize the week before you're about to post about it, that you look at inventory and that entire collection is sold out? Well, you're not going to get another big shipment of this selection. That's it. So it's like if you come out with this year we did a CoLab with Seinfeld, we did Colabs with nasa. We have no idea. If the NASA CoLab collection is just fully sold out and there's nothing we can do about it, then we're not going to waste a bunch of time and energy on social media trying to push the NASA collection sold. So then you just move on to the next thing that you have big inventory in. So what you're selling and what you're that way can also dictate the way that the marketing goes.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:49): Sure, that makes a lot of sense.\n\nPatrick McCarthy (23:50): Yeah, that's one of the behind the scenes thing that I think a lot of people wouldn't really notice.", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "21:37", "end_timestamp": "23:50", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "766b5792-eee5-4fed-b9de-03fa3e6ca5a8", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:57): So for other brands that have ambassadors, any, I guess any advice or anything that you've learned along the way that works really well for managing or working with ambassadors? What would you, I don't know, say to these other companies that are trying to start an ambassador program or looking to refine what they have?\n\nPatrick McCarthy (24:14): The way I like to do it, and it's different for everybody, I like to look at it like a family and a team element to it, and it's like, dude, get out and live it. Do it real. Go and experience what you want to. Don't try to make it look authentic. Don't try to make it look cool. Don't try to pose the person doing something. If they're not doing it, go do it. Go snowboarding, go skiing. Go do the sports that you love, document it in a really organic way and have rad team dinners together and break bread and share your moments and talk about it and be authentic and enjoy it. Enjoy the process. Enjoy the fact that you're in snowboarding or you're in skiing or you're in the mountains or you're mountain biking or whatever you're doing. Go and enjoy it, push it, do rad shit and document it and share those experiences and create a really fun team identity. (25:20): Don't just grab a bunch of athletes and pay 'em top dollar if you don't think they all get along. It takes time and energy and effort to really build a really cohesive group of ambassadors and team riders and make sure that, yeah, when you guys go on trips, it's an awesome experience. And then document that and it'll show in what you're doing and it's better than just running out and paying the next Instagram model to post about your product. That just doesn't fly in my world, but there's a space for it for sure, and everyone else is dying. You want to know why everybody's gravitating toward that right now? Because it doesn't take any work. You just sit there, you send them the box, you didn't have to do anything. They went out, pretended they were doing something cool, took a photo of themselves, posted it on there. It didn't take you anything. You didn't have to earn anything. So go out and live it, earn it. Be proud of the work that you put in and create something with real identity and authenticity or just pay somebody to do it and sit there and try to make your sales and look at yourself. I guess in the end, you want to look at yourself in the mirror and be proud of what you're doing.", "token_estimate": 613, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "23:57", "end_timestamp": "24:14", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "97c5d875-3ec5-41f3-92c5-87548ed9a4d9", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:44): Yeah, I think everyone wants to feel that way. So for the kids that are coming up and they're like when you were in your mid twenties, late twenties, and they're stoked on writing, they're great at it, but they're trying to figure out where does this take me into the future? You've obviously figured out a path from snowboarding to working on the brand side and managing team riders and all of that. What would you suggest the next generation of riders consider as they're trying to figure out, where does my snowboard career take me if they're interested in moving to the brand side.", "token_estimate": 152, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:44", "end_timestamp": "26:44", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "c9610e5a-696f-4f29-89a7-854cdc6899cd", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Patrick McCarthy (27:22): So if they're interested in parlaying it into the industry, I think the key, I mean, that's a really good question. I think that be true to yourself. Work hard when you're, work hard at it. And then, I mean, dude, for me, one of the biggest elements of it is networking. You've got to network. You've got to put yourself out there and put yourself in uncomfortable situations where you're around a bunch of people you don't know and thrive. Get to know these people get to know back in the day, for me, one of my biggest things, the reason why I am still relevant is go to trade shows, talk it up with people, meet people from different brands. Go out in the mountains, share your experiences. Don't let it all be dictated by thinking that there's some perfect algorithm to it, because there's not, the way I look at it is the more heart and soul that you pour into it, the more you're going to get out. (28:31): So I would say you're aspiring to work in the industry afterward. Then start learning about it, reading about it. Pick up some books. Think about all the different sides. What side of the industry do you want to work in? Do you want to work in marketing? Do you want to work in sales? Do you want to work in production? Do you want to work in shipping? Everybody's got a different dream. So it's like, go find what you want and learn about it, read about it, ask a lot of questions. Don't be afraid to ask questions. Don't be afraid to get uncomfortable and put yourself in weird situations that you're not normally. I always grew the most personally when I was taking myself out of my comfort zones and making myself do something that didn't always sound like exactly what I wanted to do, but what I felt like I needed to do to move on to the next stage. (29:28): So I think just networking, being true to yourself, being honest, being upfront, and ask a lot of questions, no one's going to be bummed to tell you the answers that you want to hear. And I don't think anybody ever got frustrated with somebody who asked questions. If that's what you want, put yourself in the position to go out there and get it. There's a lot of opportunities out there and everybody, there's something for everybody. So I mean, actually becoming a pro snowboarder, that takes a lot of energy and effort and guts and things. And also, you're your own brand, so you need to market yourself. You need to learn all those elements. It's insane what some kids are doing these days to grow these followings and to build themselves into a brand. They can do it a lot faster, just all sitting there, right on that little smartphone in their pocket. So yeah, I would say go out and network and put yourself in uncomfortable situations and be comfortable and be comfortable in your own skin, and believe in your own vision and the person that you are, and you follow your own heart and you'll get where you're going.", "token_estimate": 732, "speakers": ["Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "27:22", "end_timestamp": "27:22", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "ac934327-b8a5-4cea-8f90-47d0bd8ff70e", "episode_id": "380f48bd-7f19-4fa3-a43c-2a36dd307236", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483f", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (30:49): Awesome, man. Well, if there's anything to be learned, I guess from your experience, is that, yeah, that's kind of what you've done, it sounds like, and it's working out well for you. I'm stoked that you're in a good place, and we're about out of time for today, but if people want to follow along with you, I mean, I suppose they could head up to Baker and find you in the winter or up on Gal, be during the summer, but are there any social channels people can follow along at?\n\nPatrick McCarthy (31:14): Yeah, yeah, you can follow me at Patrick McCarthy on Instagram is pretty much where the majority, I mean, I'm also on Facebook soon to be TikTok, right? Yeah, I don't know. I'm not sure maybe. But yeah, I'll be in the mountains. I'll be in the lift line standing there right next to you. You just want to know it's me. But if you do see me, just say hello, and if you have any questions, hit me up. And I'm always interested in sharing and teaching anybody whatever I can. So I'm easy going and yeah, exactly. You'll see me on the trail.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:56): Awesome. Well, Patrick, thank you so much. I hope you have a good rest of your day.\n\nPatrick McCarthy (32:00): Thank you, Cole. Looking forward to hopefully getting a mountain bike in this summer.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:04): Absolutely. All right. Take care, man.\n\nPatrick McCarthy (32:06): Alright, bye.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:08): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 407, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Patrick McCarthy"], "start_timestamp": "30:49", "end_timestamp": "32:08", "podcast_index": 24, "episode_name": "Ep 24: Patrick McCarthy | Turn by Turn | 68‪6‬", "slug": "ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-24-patrick-mccarthy-turn-by-turn-686", "guest_name": "Patrick McCarthy", "guest_title": "Team Manager, 686", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Don't try to make it look authentic, don't try to make it look cool, don't try to pose the person, go do it. Do the sports you love.\"", "podcast_length": "44:46"}, {"id": "d46d5c4c-68eb-4adc-8200-bbd493ad16a5", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Adam Saraceno (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges, and the latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial, and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Let's make waves. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn, and today I'm sitting down with Adam Cino with the VP of Marketing over at Peak Design. Adam, how are you?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:46): I'm great, Cole.\n\nAdam Saraceno (00:47): Thanks for joining us. Where are you located today?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:50): I'm located down in sunny Austin, Texas.\n\nAdam Saraceno (00:54): Alright, let's jump in. So tell us a little bit about Peak Design.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:59): Well, peak Design is a product design company. We started back in 2011. We're based in San Francisco. We primarily make what we call carry products, stuff that helps you carry your stuff, and that includes backpacks and messenger bags and travel bags. It also includes a lot of camera and accessories, camera straps, clips for carrying your camera, that kind of stuff.\n\nAdam Saraceno (01:31): You guys were first kind of known, well-known for the camera stuff with the capture clip, I think the first version. Right. And then you guys have kind of grown from there, correct?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:41): That's correct. We definitely cut our teeth as a camera accessory brand, and then over the years have just kind of moved into these adjacent areas, and now there's probably more people out there that know us for our backpacks and bags than our camera gear at this point.", "token_estimate": 490, "speakers": ["Adam Saraceno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:41", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "d1e8d058-7c7e-4f51-b831-2e6b4b435bf8", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Adam Saraceno (02:01): Yep. Okay. So I'm excited to chat a little bit about your guys' history and Kickstarter and how that's all played a role. But before we dive into that, let's maybe talk a little bit about your history with the company and I guess what your journey has looked like with them.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:16): Sure. Well, our founder, Peter Dearing, was a coworker of mine in college. We bartended together at the State Barn Grill, a glorious establishment in Madison, Wisconsin that catered to an odd mixture of college students and senators because we were right next to the capitol building. But anyway, basically a college buddy of mine, Peter Deering, started Peak Design back in 2011 when he launched the capture camera clip, the original version of that on Kickstarter. And very quickly that campaign became successful. And shortly after he launched it, he called me up because at the time I was just sort of bumming around traveling, and I believe I was pet sitting for my sister while she was on her honeymoon. That was my chief responsibility back in 2011. And he called me up and he said, Hey man, I've got this business going and I know that you have a marketing background. Do you know how to build websites? And I said, no, not really. And he's like, sweet, you're hired. I think I'll be able to pay you. Was kind of the original arrangement. (03:35): So I kind helped him put together a website and market that first campaign. And by the time that campaign ended, there was 10,000 people worldwide who had pre-ordered a capture camera clip, and then we had a business to run logistics and shipping to set up and all that kind of stuff. And so at the beginning it was me and him just doing everything. And as time went on, we hired more people and I kind of settled into a marketing role at the company and that that started back in 2011. And now we are a 40 person company with a hundred different products sold all over the world. Yeah,\n\nAdam Saraceno (04:26): Very cool. What was your background prior? You said you had some marketing experience, but not a whole lot.", "token_estimate": 524, "speakers": ["Adam Saraceno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:01", "end_timestamp": "04:26", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "dbe2a41e-5b75-48b2-b220-2bb6730007d5", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (04:32): Well, I got a degree in marketing, a bachelor's degree in marketing, but I don't necessarily, I dunno, feel like I learned a ton about marketing from taking classes in college. I feel like I actually learned more from being in a fraternity and being in student organizations and all these different social things. But any who, I did spend about five years in the corporate world at a company called Intuit. They make financial management software, QuickBooks and TurboTax and stuff. (05:11): And my role there was a combination of marketing and project management. So I worked for the man for about five years, and actually I kind of realized through that that I am just not really cut out for that type of work environment. And so I quit. And that was in 2008, and then just traveled for a few years and said I would never do marketing again. And I was interning at wood shops and looking for work in the Australian Department of Conservation and all sorts of weird stuff. And then lo and behold, I ended up coming back to marketing.\n\nAdam Saraceno (05:57): Huh. Yeah, funny how that works, isn't it? Full circle. So let's talk about Kickstarter. How did that play a role in your guys' initial\n\nCole Heilborn (06:06): Success? Yeah, I mean played, it was kind of the crux of it in many ways. It essentially was our first marketing and buzz building platform and also our first sales platform. The first dollars in at peak design came through Kickstarter, and we kind of used it at the time in a bit of an unorthodox way. Peter, our founder, had essentially done all the design work and the tooling and put a lot of investment in the capture camera clip before he ever launched it on Kickstarter. And so when it was launched on Kickstarter, it was essentially ready to begin mass production. And he saw Kickstarter as less of a way to sort of validate this idea for a product and more of a way to sell a product that he was already about to start making. And I think because of that, we saw, I don't know, we were able to turn that campaign into a lot of momentum that eventually drove the business to grow.", "token_estimate": 533, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "04:32", "end_timestamp": "06:06", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "30dae6e0-f528-43ff-aae7-512d2e4a273a", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Adam Saraceno (07:30): So you guys went to Kickstarter with a pretty fully developed concept, a plan, do a lot of other businesses not go to Kickstarter? With the fully flushed out plan,\n\nCole Heilborn (07:43): Again, this was 10 years ago, and at the time I think that a lot of companies that were launching physical goods on Kickstarter were launching them in a much earlier stage. We came with a finished product. The first campaign we ran was 75 days long. And during the campaign we actually started fulfilling. We fulfilled a pre-production run of 200 units during that campaign. And I think part of the success of that campaign was that we were able to post updates of like, Hey, it's being made, check it out. We were able to give very tangible progress updates on the product, and it got people really stoked. And then once the campaign ended, it was only a matter of weeks before we started shipping these things out worldwide to 10,000 people. And so it built something that I think is kind of important on Kickstarter is especially when it's your first time launching a product to the world is establishing trust and goodwill that you, you're not just talking about this great idea, but you're also executing on it really well. And we were able to do that and show that to a bunch of people. And I think it helped us parlay a successful campaign into a business that was sort of immediately thriving, immediately profitable.\n\nAdam Saraceno (09:32): Yeah. So how did you take that trust that you earned in that first campaign and roll that into the next project and subsequent projects?", "token_estimate": 400, "speakers": ["Adam Saraceno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:30", "end_timestamp": "09:32", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "72b8b5b6-8a73-45c6-b496-ac6d93a5a10f", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (09:40): Well, a big part of it was just continuing to listen to our customers that we acquired through Kickstarter, which is actually very easy because Kickstarter gives you that very direct conduit of communication. You're very close to the people that are buying your stuff. So we listened to their feedback and we took it seriously. And the next products we developed, which were the leash and cuff, they were essentially these kind of minimalist, versatile camera straps that you could use in conjunction with your camera clip. Were sort of based on customer feedback. And then, yeah, another part of it was when we launched that second campaign, there wasn't really any precedent for companies doing that. For companies starting on Kickstarter and then going back to Kickstarter to relaunch products. There wasn't a playbook for that. And we were really concerned actually that are people going to see this as us kind of going back to the honey hole.\n\nAdam Saraceno (10:57): Oh, interesting.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:58): And were they going to see it somehow as US Gold digging and us using this platform in a way that it wasn't meant to be used? And we were really, really sensitive about that. And we were very upfront with why we were going back to Kickstarter. We were very transparent and it was just like, Hey, this worked really well for us when we launched our first product and we made all these fantastic customers through it, and we see no reason why we shouldn't do that again. And people were extremely and immediately very stoked to see us back there. We didn't hear a peep of, I don't know, resentment from Kickstarter or from any of our customers. And so we kind of at that point realized, Hey, maybe this is a sustainable way to keep growing our business.\n\nAdam Saraceno (12:02): I didn't know that you guys were one of the first companies that kind of adopted that model. And now Kickstarter has become a core component of each product launch that you've had since, right?", "token_estimate": 502, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "09:40", "end_timestamp": "12:02", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "375a12ff-bd34-4ac1-99d0-e67335ec361a", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:14): Yep. Every major product launch we've done has been on Kickstarter, and we've done one Kickstarter a year since 2011. And if I were to forecast, I'd say we were probably going to do another one this year.\n\nAdam Saraceno (12:32): I guess beyond the immediate benefits of Kickstarter, why do you guys continue to go back?\n\nCole Heilborn (12:38): Well, I'd say it's equal parts, a financial tool for us, a cashflow management tool and a marketing tool. The cashflow part is, so peak design is we don't have any outside funding. We don't have any outside investors. We occasionally take on small loans here and there when need be, but we're essentially entirely funded by our own revenue. And Kickstarter gives us the ability when we are doing a big product launch and we need to make a huge product development investment and all the tooling expenses and all the manufacturing expenses for that first production run, (13:38): It essentially allows us to acquire that cash upfront via a presale so that we can, it just allows us to manage our money that way. And you can tell how much I actually know about financial management by the fact that I'm stumbling over the simplest of explanation of how businesses work. But yeah, become a very kind of important part of our month to month financial management. And a lot of our relationships with our suppliers now are sort of built on this trust of when we launch this product, we're going to have this huge injection of pre-order cash to kind of pay them with. So that's like the financial part. The marketing part is just, there is, I guess, bang for your buck. I can't think of a higher ROI more impactful way to build awareness and buzz around a new product. For us, at least for a company that makes consumer goods, especially in the space that we're in, we make kind of these high-end products that are photo video related, that are targeted towards higher end consumers of everyday carry gear and people that live in cities and just people that are concentrated in high numbers on the Kickstarter platform.", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "12:14", "end_timestamp": "12:38", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "7cb67dfa-cabd-445a-a8da-05ff507c6401", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Adam Saraceno (15:26): So what's from your first campaign to your more recent campaigns, what's one thing that you've kept and repeated one principle, and what's one thing that you've changed from things you've learned?\n\nCole Heilborn (15:44): That's a good one. Captain repeated is definitely the transparency, and it's been something that has just, I think because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly just how much, being transparent sometimes too, a fault is beloved by your customers. And it's just like, seems like, I mean, it's kind of a no-brainer, but it's just a better, easier way to run a business when you're brutally honest about everything that you do. And so whether it's transparency about keeping a high degree of update about the status of development of a product and boots on the ground footage from our factories and that kind of stuff to transparency about when we fuck up. There's been a few occasions where we've launched a product and then later discovered some sort of manufacturing defect. And I think through Kickstarter, we've learned how important it is to just be very honest about it. And in terms of things that we've changed, that is a good one. We've certainly, over the years, I'd say developed and sharpened technically our video production capabilities and just our abilities to tell a really good compelling story. And that's kind of been a very natural, organic progression. And it's how all companies do it. And\n\nAdam Saraceno (18:02): Yeah, I think when I go to Kickstarter and I'm looking at a new campaign, one of the first things I do is check out the video. How important is the video, I guess, what are the things that you're thinking through as you're creating it that are necessary for a successful project launch?", "token_estimate": 445, "speakers": ["Adam Saraceno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:26", "end_timestamp": "18:02", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "a7ddec34-16ba-4cee-8faf-019350507b93", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:22): The video is probably the most important aspect of a Kickstarter campaign. It is your opportunity to quickly and succinctly explain to somebody why they should buy your product or support your project. And ultimately, in my opinion, the thing that makes a Kickstarter successful is actually not the video. It's the thing that you're making or selling itself. You have to start with a good idea that has been executed on well and is just kind of business 1 0 1. It's a product that has to address a need that enough people have and are willing to pay for a solution for. And I do think that there are a lot of campaigns out there that end up being not that successful, even though they have a really good polished video, it's just kind of like, well, it's just not something that there's a huge appetite for. But when it comes to the video, it's just kind of like marketing 1 0 1. I wouldn't say there's a silver bullet. It's explaining to people what it is you're making and help 'em understand it and help 'em understand how it's going to make their life better. And then maybe share also the story about who you are and why you're making it. (20:07): And yeah, I think that the storytelling is always an important part, but not as important as the thing itself.\n\nAdam Saraceno (20:18): What you're saying is it's important to really focus in on what you're doing, what you're solving, rather than the messaging that's going around the product that you're launching.", "token_estimate": 373, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "18:22", "end_timestamp": "20:18", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "186aa2a2-d3e1-4494-b707-a1ba4b5f9a87", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (20:28): Yeah, I mean, I think it's easy to give into, especially after you watch a lot of Kickstarter videos and you're seeing, all right, here's what all these different companies are doing. Saying, I think it's easy to want to start your Kickstarter video off with this kind of high level. If you're making a travel bag to say travel, it is such a wonderful way to see wax eloquent on why travel is important and why we all care about it and all the great things it's brought to us. And then use that as sort of this lead in this grandiose lead in to be like, and that's why we developed the perfect tool, but at the end of the day, people back your campaign of the thing. (21:24): And so, I don't know. I guess one more kind of specific piece of advice and something we've tried to do more and more over the years is just cut the BS and get right to it. Here's this tripod we made, here's why it's better than the other tripods that we've used, and here's how it works. Here's all the features. And for us, for our type of customer who is a pretty tech savvy, gear minded person, people really like seeing the details. So we definitely try to spare no detail when we're talking about the products and the materials and when we're providing Kickstarter updates, like the thought behind why we use this type of aluminum versus another type of aluminum and the machining process that it takes to make this specific part and all that kind of stuff, that helps build the story as well.\n\nAdam Saraceno (22:24): Okay. So you guys have come out with a few different types of products, I think is your most successful one. The Messenger bag,\n\nCole Heilborn (22:34): I guess our most successful product to date is the one that we launched most recently in 2019, the peak design travel tripod.", "token_estimate": 450, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "20:28", "end_timestamp": "22:34", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "13197fcf-6895-46c8-a41d-f87c522fb3cc", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Adam Saraceno (22:42): Okay. Okay, cool. So you've come out with the tripod, the messenger bag, both of those are products that the consumer is already familiar with the tripod in a bag. Those markets are already, there's already products out there, but with a capture clip, as far as I know, that was kind of a new product that people didn't know they needed. What are some, I guess, tips or just thoughts on how to bring an entirely new product to market, something that people aren't aware that they need?", "token_estimate": 127, "speakers": ["Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "22:42", "end_timestamp": "22:42", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "aff487d8-faf7-42af-a003-120187b23a81", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:14): Yeah, that is a good point. The capture camera clip is really the only product that we've launched on Kickstarter, where it was a new category, camera clips did not exist prior to that. And I think the first thing was, and this was prior to me joining the company, but Peter or founder, when he kind of had the idea for this camera carrying device, it was very much based on his own traveling with A-D-S-L-R, him carrying it with a strap, having it bang on his neck, having it be a pain in the ass to carry when you're riding a moped or backpacking or something like that, and sort of devising the solution and then using it himself for during his travel, using it for months, and just testing it, getting that immediate feedback from people in state Park. It's like, what the hell is that? How are you carrying your camera right now? I guess the first part is just bench test the solution that you're making and ensure that it is solving a real need for yourself. B, it's solving a real need for other people like you. (24:40): And just sort of validating that this new product and this new category is actually that you're onto something, that it is a good idea and that you've executed on it well. And then from the marketing perspective, from when you're telling the story to people was, I don't know, it was really just about, Hey, you're familiar with solutions A, B, and C for carrying your camera. Here's why all of these things are inadequate for these types of activities, and here's why this solution is actually a much better one. And so it's, I dunno, again, pretty classic marketing, just problem definition, and then explaining how your solution solves the problem in a better way, and what are the benefits that you can get from that.\n\nAdam Saraceno (25:45): Yeah, so there's a variety of different types of crowdfunding sites out there. How do you choose which one to go with?\n\nCole Heilborn (25:53): Kickstarter has always been, in our opinion, the gold standard. I don't know if they were the first. I assume they were.\n\nAdam Saraceno (26:03): I mean, they're just like the biggest name I feel, so I would assume they were first.", "token_estimate": 543, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "23:14", "end_timestamp": "26:03", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "20e81507-584d-4fda-8244-ff3d7ac905a9", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:07): Yeah, they certainly feel the biggest I, and I'd say Indiegogo is the other kind of big one that people talk about. We have used them, we use them for a post Kickstarter kind of momentum building campaign, and we had okay results there, but we, Kickstarter just kind of feels like the biggest, the most robust in terms of feature set and stuff like that. And I don't know, I also, Kickstarters just sort of, they have a zeitgeist around their community that is very sort of creative focused, and they're a B Corp. They have a very clear mission to leave the world a better place than they found it, and so we continue to gravitate towards them.\n\nAdam Saraceno (27:25): Yeah. Yeah. No, that's cool. I didn't know they were B Corp. That's awesome. It's cool to see that sticker pop up more and more, and also climate neutral, which I know you guys have played a big role in getting off the ground.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:37): Totally.\n\nAdam Saraceno (27:38): But anyways, that's another conversation. Do you need any other partnerships to bring a successful Kickstarter to fruition?\n\nCole Heilborn (27:47): No, I don't think so. Our first campaign we did, we ran solo and we did a little bit of, we tried to hack our way through Facebook advertising and hack our way through PR and that kind of stuff. We got lucky with a few big PR hits that I don't think we had any sort of actual personal hand in generating. They were just sort of organic.\n\nAdam Saraceno (28:21): They make you look a lot better, right?\n\nCole Heilborn (28:23): Yeah, absolutely. But no, I don't think you need to have to be consuming certain third party services in order to have a successful campaign. We certainly leverage a ton of them now, though.\n\nAdam Saraceno (28:41): That's not something, if a new company is looking at launching, they don't need to be like, okay, we need to have our PR agency. We need to have our do. It really just is all about the idea and how you execute.", "token_estimate": 494, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "26:07", "end_timestamp": "28:41", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "a694377a-3cdf-48ea-95c8-c32999de8614", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:53): Yeah, I think so. I mean, things like PR and digital advertising are certainly big and important levers to pull in a campaign. And if you can drum up the resources to do those things, they can certainly pay dividends, but I would never consider them a must have. And I think that's part of the beauty of Kickstarter as a platform is that peak design, a 40 person business. We use Kickstarter to launch our products, and anybody can have access to that same basic toolkit.\n\nAdam Saraceno (29:57): Yeah. That kind of leads me to my next question. I mean, what you're describing kind of sounds like it's too good to be true. There's this program out there, you can launch a product if it goes well, you can retain a hundred percent ownership, you get financing marketing, but it doesn't always work out for a lot of projects, a lot of companies. What do people get wrong about crowdfunding?", "token_estimate": 228, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Adam Saraceno"], "start_timestamp": "28:53", "end_timestamp": "29:57", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "50c1c167-281e-4a1c-9ee5-5aa79988eb41", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (30:22): Yeah, that's a good point. I mean, I'm pretty sure that most Kickstarter projects are unsuccessful. If you look at just all the numbers, there've been more unsuccessful ones than successful ones, I think by a significant margin. The things that sort of the most high profile or common kind of mistakes that I see made a big one is around overpromising. And that can be caused by a few different sources. It can just be caused by maybe the idea, maybe the product that you have is just either not that great, or the execution on it has been not that great, the design, the construction or whatever, and you've hyped something up then, but the pictures and what you're actually showing is evidence of this thing aren't really supporting that hype. And I think people see through that pretty quickly. Another thing is, and this is something that is actually not necessarily keeps projects from initially succeeding, but keeps people with successful projects from growing that into a successful business, is basically not getting your ducks in a row from a product development and manufacturing standpoint (32:05): Early enough. Like I said, when we launched that first campaign, we were essentially already ready to press go on production. And it's not like that was a conscious strategy. It was more just out of this naivety of like, well, yeah, if we're going to sell a product, we better be ready to make it. And I think there's a lot of people that go to Kickstarter more so to validate an idea than to sell kind of a concrete thing that they're already ready to make. So a lot of campaigns, I mean, the coolest cooler, if you remember that it was, no, I don't. It was like Kickstarter's most funded project for a while, and it was a cooler with all these bells and whistles on it with a Bluetooth speaker and a built-in blender to make your pina coladas and stuff like that. And they had this huge campaign, and then it took them years, years to fulfill because they weren't ready for their success, and they did not kind of get their manufacturing together with enough time. They were also not great at communicating with their backers about it, and it just kind of turned into this angry mob with pitchforks in their comments section on their Kickstarter page. (33:47): So yeah, I think that that's a big area where I see, I would suggest folks that are, especially if you're bringing a thing, a consumer product to life, really try and get that stuff in order as early as you can.", "token_estimate": 627, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:22", "end_timestamp": "30:22", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "a1b13286-da51-488d-949c-394b6d86f4e2", "episode_id": "94c732f0-738f-4a0c-aae1-30e2428b36e6", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483c", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Adam Saraceno (34:07): Yeah. So we're about out of time for today, but what are you most excited for looking forward into peak design's future?\n\nCole Heilborn (34:16): Gee, there's all sorts of stuff.\n\nAdam Saraceno (34:20): Maybe you could tell us what some of the new products are.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:23): Well, yeah, until we get our IP poop in a group, I can't unfortunately talk too much about that. I am pretty stoked about our upcoming product launch that will happen via Kickstarter sometime this fall. I'm also stoked for, as you mentioned before, climate neutral that we co-founded last year with a company called BioLite. And they're, it's a nonprofit organization, has essentially made it super easy for any type of business to become carbon neutral. And it is catching on like wildfire right now, which is a bad analogy to use because wildfire is a result of climate change. And this is exactly what the climate neutrals is sort of established on the assumption, the idea that together businesses and consumers can actually take very simple steps that if done on a large scale would have a sizable impact on reversing the effects of climate change, and that we don't need to wait for governments to step in and do stuff. So I'm stoked for climate neutral.\n\nAdam Saraceno (35:41): Awesome. Well, Adam, thank you so much. If people want to follow along, either with Peak design or with you, where can they go to find you guys\n\nCole Heilborn (35:49): Your social media platform of choice? If you were to pick one? I would start with Instagram Peak Design on Instagram. Yeah, that's where our happenings are posted on a daily basis.\n\nAdam Saraceno (36:03): Thank you so much. Thank you for sharing your experiences and your perspective. It's been super educational. I really appreciate it.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:10): Thanks, Cole. Appreciate it.\n\nAdam Saraceno (36:12): Yeah, take care.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:13): You too.\n\nAdam Saraceno (36:14): Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Adam Saraceno", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "34:07", "end_timestamp": "36:14", "podcast_index": 25, "episode_name": "Ep 25: Adam Saraceno | Kickstarter 101 | Peak Desig‪n", "slug": "ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-25-adam-saraceno-kickstarter-101-peak-design", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Because of our Kickstarter roots, we learned very quickly, how much being transparent, sometimes to a fault is beloved by your community.\"", "podcast_length": "44:47"}, {"id": "9b87a12b-16e5-4142-ad5f-68d78b07bee3", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling the fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Porch Line Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Let's make waves. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Erica Rosen, the VP of Marketing at BioLite. Erica, how are you?\n\nEric Rosen (00:45): I'm doing great today. How are you\n\nCole Heilborn (00:47): Doing? Fantastic. Where are you joining us from today?\n\nEric Rosen (00:50): I am over in Brooklyn, New York, where BioLite is headquartered.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:56): Okay, nice. I think you're the first guest from New York City and I think even New York State, so congrats.\n\nEric Rosen (01:02): Yeah, thank you. Yeah, it's funny, I think a lot of people are surprised when they hear BioLite is headquartered in New York City, but there's actually a really thriving outdoor scene here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:13): I think the strongest sources of people usually come from Colorado, like the Pacific Northwest, like California, Oregon, and then that's really about it. So thanks for breaking the mold.\n\nEric Rosen (01:27): Yeah, happy to be here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:28): So tell us a little bit about BioLite. Who are you, what do you do, and tell us a little bit about your background too.", "token_estimate": 435, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eric Rosen"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:28", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "38b9aefa-ff73-4230-aec0-221936132b38", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Eric Rosen (01:34): Yeah, definitely. So I'll start with BioLite first. For those who are unfamiliar with BioLite, we're a social enterprise that is out to totally change the way that people relate to their energy access and energy use. That's like a big, lofty kind of the reason we get up and go to work every day. How that translates into how listeners of the podcast might interact with BioLite is we make outdoor energy equipment. We make stoves, lights, solar panels, batteries, all pieces of personal equipment that run exclusively off of renewable energy so that you can create a level of energy independence in any off-grid environment.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:17): And you guys, remind me of the name of the stove that you guys have, but I feel like that's the one that everyone knows you guys for.\n\nEric Rosen (02:23): Yeah. Yeah. It's funny in terms of throwback trivia, one day I hope to be a question on a bar night trivia. Let's aim high guys. But a little bit of trivia for BioLite is we originally launched as bio light stoves because our flagship product was a unit called the Light Camp Stove, and it is a portable wood-burning camp stove that actually generates electricity from the heat of its own fire.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:52): That's so cool.\n\nEric Rosen (02:54): Yeah, so it's got this patented design inside of it that uses something called thermoelectrics. So like what a solar panel does with the sun. This does with heat, so it takes a little bit of waste heat from the fire, converts it into usable electricity. That power then does two things. One, it runs an internal fan that blows air back into the burn chamber, so you're actually burning smokeless, and then the surplus power goes into a USB port so that you can do things like charge your headlamp or a battery or your phone.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:24): Yeah, gotcha. I remember when that came out and I was like, how does that work? That's so crazy. And it's all in this little compact unit. It's no bigger than a water bottle.", "token_estimate": 501, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:34", "end_timestamp": "03:24", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "c50fa990-4a69-4adb-9ca1-2cfdd9fadc4e", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Eric Rosen (03:34): Exactly. Yeah. It's like the size of an Nalgene, and when you look at it, the power pack actually comes off of the burn chamber and can nest inside of the stove when it's not in use. So it's actually a pretty small footprint. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (03:47): Gotcha. So you started this BioLite stove. You guys have changed a lot since then. I guess. Where are you guys now?\n\nEric Rosen (03:55): Yeah, so we came out as BioLite stove, but we always knew that we were an energy company. From what I said at the beginning, we always knew that we were an energy company, but what's been kind of the coolest part of the last eight years of the company is watching the reasons to believe that we're an energy company come to life. And so the way that I think those reasons to believe have really manifested is twofold. One, our portfolio of products. So we went from stoves to solar panels, lights, lights in terms of both lanterns and headlamps, power banks. And so a really robust suite of products that really help you understand that we are looking at all of the different need states of energy. And then secondly, our impact. So the amount of people that we have reached in those eight years. And so one thing that I mentioned at the top is that we're a social enterprise. (04:53): So again, lots of folks who are listening, you'll be engaging with BioLite mostly through the lens of the outdoor recreational industry, but half of our business. So I said that I'm headquartered here in Brooklyn. We actually have a second headquarter, and it's in Nairobi. Half of our team is actually based in East Africa because a whole half of our business is dedicated to the same type of r and d and technological development to deliver safe off-grid energy access to those who are off-grid, not by choice, but by circumstance. And so over the last eight years, we've really built out a product portfolio in emerging markets as well as a distribution network in emerging markets to reach hundreds of thousands of households. And so we're really able to deliver on that promise of a future where clean energy can be accessed and shared by everybody.", "token_estimate": 539, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:34", "end_timestamp": "03:55", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "1f796db4-5e79-4846-9e6d-e7ef71be6515", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (05:47): Wow. So break it down a little bit. How are you guys helping people who are off grid, not by choice, but by circumstance? What does that look like?\n\nEric Rosen (05:55): Yeah, so I guess to zoom out for one second, people might be like, whoa, that's crazy. You have two totally different audiences. But what's really fascinating is that when you are in an off-grid environment, be it deep in the back country or in a rural household, your needs states actually end up becoming very similar. You need to cook reliably, you need to light your night after the sun goes down, and you need ways to charge the devices around you. You need access to choose how and where you use your energy. And most notably, that manifests often in communication, for example, just being able to charge your phone. And so those need states exist in both markets, but they look a little bit different. So in emerging markets where often rural households don't have access to energy, it means two things. One, they're either using really expensive and often dangerous fuel sources. So cooking over open indoor fires, buying kerosene, which is often not regulated. And so the prices are really high or the fuel's not good or anything like that. Or unfortunately, if you pay a premium when there isn't an easy solution to access. And so a very specific example of that is that rates of cell phone ownership actually outpace rates of electrification in a lot of Sub-Saharan Africa, which\n\nSpeaker 3 (07:24): Means,\n\nEric Rosen (07:25): Yeah. So it means that you have households that own a cell phone but actually don't have a way to charge it. And so what that means is that often they end up going into town and they have to pay somebody to charge their phone and the amount that they pay, literally when you break it down to dollars per watts, it is highway robbery. The amount that we pay is a fraction of a cent every time we plug our phone in at the end of the night, and they're paying just multiples of that to power their phone. Whoa. Yeah. So that, sorry, I just spent the first two minutes articulating the problem. But in terms of,\n\nSpeaker 3 (08:03): That's great.", "token_estimate": 543, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Eric Rosen", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "05:47", "end_timestamp": "08:03", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "1a3e5288-0e70-4535-b662-5b069065b556", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Eric Rosen (08:06): In terms of the solution, BioLite is taking the same type of core technologies that we're developing in our outdoor markets and turning them into products for emerging markets. And so the two main products that come to mind are, I talked about the cam stove. We have a product called the Home Stove. And the home stove is the same core technology, but it's just a larger scale, and it's meant for everyday cooking, and it has the same ability to generate power to power an LED light or a cell phone, something like that. And then the other product that's super exciting is what we call the solar home six 20. And so this is, it fits into the size of a shoebox and it's a solar panel, a control box with a kind of power bank and power storage system, and then three hanging lights. And this can transform any off-grid home into an electrified house.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:59): Wow. Year to date, how many houses or do you measure in houses people? How many people have you helped?\n\nEric Rosen (09:07): Oh, when is this going to air? Because I have exciting milestones to share.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:13): I think this podcast will be coming out late September.", "token_estimate": 294, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "08:06", "end_timestamp": "09:13", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "d76241b6-af04-46ff-977c-d8dc8ddf599a", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Eric Rosen (09:16): Cool. Well, I can then I'll say that in emerging markets, wink, wink, nudge, nudge, we have helped close to a million people, but you should probably check back in the late fall and see what that number is. But yeah, I mean, we are achieving scale. I think that it's one thing for us to talk about this amazing journey and then be like, and we've helped tens of people and listen, helping tens of people, that's where you need to start. That's absolutely where you need to start. But I think that for us, in terms of being able to fulfill this promise and fulfill this mission, really understanding that the work we do needs to be not only sustainable, but scalable really sits at the heart of why we do this. And we hold ourselves accountable to that. Every April we release an impact report. And so to your point when this is over, I'll send you the impact report, but we do measure by household, and then what you're able to do is kind of extrapolate the number of people impacted by just multiplying it by average number of people in a household.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:21): Sure, sure. So when you guys first started, did you start knowing that this is ultimately what you wanted to do? Did you start as an outdoor recreation brand and then realized the opportunity that existed?", "token_estimate": 325, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:16", "end_timestamp": "10:21", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "97ccedfe-cc53-4b8b-b017-2c3ee6d3cd37", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Eric Rosen (10:32): Trick question. Neither. We started as a random nights and weekend project. So to go a little bit into the kind of origin story of Viol, we were co-founded by Jonathan Cedar and Alec Drummond, who were two product designers who were both based in New York, but were avid outdoors people and loved being outside. And they complained that, and they would take camping trips together and they would complain often that they had to use gas canisters. And it felt like this cognitive dissonance of being outside in the outdoors and then you're lugging gas canisters with you, it just felt like there was a break in the experience. And they really liked the idea of trying to cook over wood and use biomass fuel. But as everybody's experiences, there's smoke. And so they were like, how could we burn more clean and everything like that. And so they developed the prototype for the cam stove, and then they got some interest in it and some traction. And then they actually went to something called the Ethos Clean Combustion Conference. And it's all about wood cooking\n\nSpeaker 4 (11:44): That", "token_estimate": 277, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Speaker 4"], "start_timestamp": "10:32", "end_timestamp": "11:44", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "f1b59f10-7b4e-4b5a-be54-456a97d3271c", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Eric Rosen (11:44): It exists. It's in the Pacific Northwest. And so they went with this little crazy prototype and they thought they were going to find other tinkerers and folks working out of their garage. They just thought they were kind of going to an affinity group just going to like, Hey, this is our version of Comic-Con, but with moving parts. And so they went there and what they found instead, were public health experts, scientists, engineers, all talking about the fact that we need a solution for better biomass combustion because half the planet still cooks over smokey open fires every day, and it kills, it actually kills more than 4 million people annually. So it's one of the biggest causes of death, and nobody talks about it just for perspective. It kills more people than aids, TB and malaria combined annually. Indoor air pollution is just devastating. And so that was a moment where it went from being like, oh, we have this kooky little toy for the outdoors to, oh, actually we have a technology whose application could impact billions of people. And so it went from like, here's this crazy little thing. Maybe we could just launch it and see what happens to, oh, we actually have an opportunity for scale and a reason for being that really anchors this in the ability to build a business. And by business, I mean social enterprise, but really building impact at the core of what we do.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:22): Yeah, I mean that right there helps you guys. That was your why. That's where it kind of all came together, it sounds like.\n\nEric Rosen (13:29): Yeah, exactly.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:31): So with your job in particular, I guess, how are you guys split up? You have the North American headquarters and then you have the African headquarters. Do you work with that team, or how do you guys balance and get stuff done?", "token_estimate": 462, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:44", "end_timestamp": "13:31", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "f2b284c5-6a63-4aba-96a7-a1f17a9adf6c", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Eric Rosen (13:47): Yeah, it's a lot of Google Sheets and a lot of Zoom, but yeah, we have two headquarters, but we really do strive to be a single team and really be in communication with each other. I am the VP of marketing, and I do oversee all global marketing, but I think that an important thing to know is what I don't know. And so a big part of my job is collaborating really closely with my team in Nairobi to understand the blind spots that the New York based team might have and make sure that we're understanding things like language differences, understanding that photography shot in Kenya is not going to work in Senegal, which sounds obvious, but it's a really important conversation to continually be having. And so yeah, I think that we do work really closely together. We do work on campaigns together. The team, the marketing team that's based in New York is basically operates like an in-house creative agency. So we do all of our packaging design for both markets. We work on all of our web presence for both markets, so both websites. And so yeah, we really do sit across both sides of the business. And I think that one thing that's nice about that is it really enables us to have a super consistent brand and a super consistent presence in terms of how we show up in both markets.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:27): And that's a great lead into my next question, which is what type of power does a brand have? I mean, here you guys are leading this charge. How does that make you feel and what does that allow you to do?", "token_estimate": 384, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:47", "end_timestamp": "15:27", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "4098fbaf-cde8-44d5-bb51-4a1412f52ded", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Eric Rosen (15:41): What power does a brand have? Yeah, I mean, I would say that the simplest answer, which could then turn into an eight hour conversation is it's a way to build trust and measure permission. Interview over, no, I'm just kidding. Yeah, I would say that for brands, number one, really using your brand or not using, but just building your brand as a way to build trust among your customers helps create that affinity and helps create recall, and then more importantly, helps protect you against competition. And it really does mean that, listen, for BioLite for example, we're always going to want to be as affordable as possible, but we also know that our products, they cost money. And so we want you to feel like you're getting valued. We want you to feel like you're getting a great experience. And so I think that building trust and that kind of routinized and reliable association is really one way to really leverage and build your brand. And then in terms of permission, which is the kind of corollary of that is that if a brand wants to be around for a long time, we might want to expand into new categories or we might want to look at new offerings. And some of those offerings are going to be more competitive than others. And so what I would say is by building a strong brand, it's not even necessarily that you get more permission to do more things, but it's that you get really good feedback on where you have permission to go.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:23): Okay. Interesting. Yeah, no, that's really well said. And I think that's interesting that the permission aspect, because I think a lot of people would describe a brand as trust and reputation, but I haven't heard many people describe it as permission. Also, how does that affect what you guys do at light that permission?", "token_estimate": 451, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:41", "end_timestamp": "17:23", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "4002ebdf-a689-4828-8d1a-8fd0600cb991", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Eric Rosen (17:46): I think that, I'm trying to think about a good example. So actually I think our headlamps a really good example. So BioLite started, we started making stoves, we made solar panels, we made power banks, and then a couple of years ago, we decided that we wanted to get into the headlamp space because BioLite has built trust and built a reputation of being a brand that solves problems. We look at problems and then we take a user and design centered approach to solve those problems through our design and technology. I think that, so as I think people have had really good experiences with our camp stove, they've had really good experiences with our solar panel in each of those, part of those experiences involved solving a problem and understanding that there were underserved needs in terms of the customer's experience. And so when we went into the headlamp market, the headlamp market is crowded and it is filled with incumbents who have been doing this for a long time. (18:56): And fast forward two years later, we are a big part of the market. We've taken market share, we have tremendous positive media coverage for the headlamp, and more importantly, we have hundreds of thousands of them out in the market. And so I think that that is a great example of if you 10 years ago were like, oh, filets going to make stoves and headlamps, we'd be like, good luck. Good luck stealing somebody else's lunch. But I think that the trust and the experience that we were able to build with people about, we build stuff that works. We build stuff that's intuitive and we build stuff that solves problems, gave us permission to really succeed and have our customers take a chance on trying out our headlamps.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:46): Yeah. So I feel like your role with the company, you have a really exciting opportunity because the company's been around for a while. You have some great success, but there's really like you're looking towards the future and you're like, there's so many places we could go. Where do you see the BioLite brand going and how are you working to get there?", "token_estimate": 526, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:46", "end_timestamp": "19:46", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}, {"id": "ffa20315-4278-426c-94db-1170a8b7ec5a", "episode_id": "1e32aa47-8f7f-4b63-9bdd-4e65200e4830", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c483e", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Eric Rosen (20:08): Yeah, I mean, I think that where we are going is we want to continue. One of the things that we talk about is helping people transition to a renewable energy future, one wat at a time. That's what we talk about. And the one watt at a time is really important because we say it because it really represents the need of small personal daily interactions. And so that's where we started. We started with these small three watt, five watt, 10 watt interactions. But in terms of where BioLite is going in the future, if really want to be a part of your life and we want to be a part of a household name, we've got to get into more parts of your life. We can't be the single, you interact with us on one camping trip per year. And so I think that you're already seeing that. I think that the headlamp is something that you're going to use multiple times a year, whether it's out on a camping trip or in a power outage, or like me looking in the corner cabinet in my kitchen that is just a black hole. So I think that's one. And then I think another one is when we launched the fire pit. So I think the fire pit was a very noteworthy expansion of BioLite from the back country into your backyard. And I think that that's kind of the things that we're thinking about is how can we continue to build out a portfolio of products that really help you understand that your daily engagement with energy can be built in renewables.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:42): Yeah, interesting. You mentioned wanting to become a household name. I guess two questions. Why is that and how does that help your mission?", "token_estimate": 401, "speakers": ["Eric Rosen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:08", "end_timestamp": "21:42", "podcast_index": 27, "episode_name": "Ep 27: Erica Rosen | One Watt at a Time | BioLit‪e‬", "slug": "ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-27-erica-rosen-one-watt-at-a-time-biolite", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"A brand is a way to build trust and measure permission.\"", "podcast_length": "44:49"}]
